The 6 ft 2'' pro, who slammed 29 winners from his forehand side, and saved all but one breakpoint in the match, played the big points better. He will play second seed Rafael Nadal in the last eight on Tuesday.

After closing out on his second match point, Tiafoe looked at his box and simply shook his head. His expression dazed. Then the celebrations began, channeling another LeBron James act, coming right out of the LA Lakers' Arena, the 21-year-old pulled off his shirt, slapped his biceps and let out a yell that echoed across Melbourne Park before going down on his knees. .

"This is crazy man," the 21-year-old kept saying, shaking his head in disbelief. His phone is blowing up with messages and everyone wants a piece him. "ESPN put a pretty cool picture of me and LeBron James, a video of us doing his celebration. The caption is, 'Who did it better?'," Tiafoe explained.

As ATP's next generation star made his way to the centre for the on-court interview, he broke down, weeping into his tee. "I told my parents 10 years ago that I would change their life, and here I am at 21, in the quarterfinals of a Grand Slam."

The American, the son of Sierra Leonean immigrants, was raised at a USTA regional training centre in Maryland, where his father (Frances Sr) first worked on the construction site and then as the head of maintenance. He was later given a spare office in the facility to live and Frances and his twin brother Franklin lived with him. Their mother Alphina worked as a nurse.

"Mine isn't a normal tennis story. In the beginning of my career, I was playing for them, trying to do everything for my family. Now I put them in a great place, I'm trying to do it for me now," he said. "In March 2017, I bought a house in Maryland for my mom. My dad is in an apartment in Orlando. That was big. Getting us out of you know, put us in better situations."

"In my academy, there were a ton of wealthy guys, you got cars rolling in there with chauffeurs. That gave me an incentive, a reason to work every day," he said.

It was a trip to Sierra Leone when he was eight that helped Tiafoe appreciate what he had. Embrace the opportunity.

"My pops wanted me to go there, he thought I was getting spoilt. People used to make fun of us for wearing physical education shirts to play tennis, holes in our shoes. Pops told us you guys don't even understand. You got American passports, you got the opportunity to do something great. Go and do it. That put things in perspective for me."

'

