b'Mumbai: The stock price of engineering & construction major L&T dipped over 3% early in Monday\xe2\x80\x99s trading after markets regulator Sebi rejected its plans for a Rs 9,000-crore buyback, citing unusually high debt-equity ratio if the share purchase is successful. In mid-session, the stock, however, rallied from its early lows and was up nearly 1.5% on reports that the company could announce a dividend of Rs 53 per share. It finally ended at Rs 1,314, down a marginal 0.3% by close of Monday\xe2\x80\x99s trade on the BSE.

On Saturday, in a filing to the BSE, L&T said Sebi had asked it not to proceed with the proposal as its total debt after the buyback (assuming full acceptance) could translate to more than two times its equity capital and free reserves on a consolidated basis and hence the scheme was not in compliance with the Companies Act and Sebi rules. In other words, the company\xe2\x80\x99s debt-equity ratio will exceed 2:1 post a fully successful buyback.



