The agency has maintained a stable outlook on the finances of the states for FY20.

The aggregate fiscal deficit of the states will come in higher at 3.2% in FY20, which is higher than the FY19 midyear outlook forecast of 2.8%, it says, adding \xe2\x80\x9calthough this is higher than the fiscally prudent level of 3%, this will not pose any significant upside risks to these states' aggregate debt burden in FY20.\xe2\x80\x9d

On expenditure side, the agency expects states\xe2\x80\x99 aggregate revenue expenditure to grow 18.9% to Rs 33.28 trillion in FY20. \xe2\x80\x9cThe announcement of farm loan waivers by MP, Chhattisgarh, Assam and Rajasthan last month extends the list of states that have resorted to this mechanism to address farmers\xe2\x80\x99 distress. Additionally, Odisha and Jharkhand also announced schemes to provide financial assistance to small and marginal farmers along the lines of the Rythu Bandhu scheme in Telangana,\xe2\x80\x9d it said.

The agency expects states' aggregate capex/GDP to come in marginally lower at 3% in FY20 from the budget estimate of 3.07% for FY19. \xe2\x80\x9cThis capex/GDP is expected to come in below 3% for Tamil Nadu, Haryana, Bengal and Kerala in FY20,\xe2\x80\x9d it said.

It also expects the aggregate debt/GDP to rise to 25.1% in FY20 from the budgeted 24.3% for FY19. \xe2\x80\x9cWe do not view the increase to be detrimental to states\xe2\x80\x99 debt sustainability position, although the states would channelise some part of borrowings towards meeting revenue expenditure,\xe2\x80\x9d the report said. According to the agency, MP, Tamil Nadu and Kerala are most susceptible to see an increase in debt in FY20. The agency estimates gross market borrowings of states to be Rs 5.7 trillion in FY20. 

