Industry watchers TOI spoke to say a combination of cost-effectiveness and the Indian penchant for verbal communication has resulted in voice calls holding their ground in the country amid dwindling relevance globally. Further, with data itself not as strong and flawless as is needed for VoIP (Voice over Internet Protocol) calls, smartphone owners say they often rely on regular calls for a better experience.

\xe2\x80\x9cIt is mainly a function of Indian consumer behaviour. People are used to the convenience of dialling a call, and a growing number of users are getting into the voice mode with the hindrance of cost also going away,\xe2\x80\x9d Faisal Kawoosa, founder of tech and telecom consulting firm techARC, said. He added that, with over 500 million feature phones in the Indian market, we cannot write off voice services in India yet.

The feature phone market is also growing. According to IDC, vendors in India shipped a total of 56 million feature phones in the fourth quarter of 2017, making it \xe2\x80\x9cthe highest-ever shipments\xe2\x80\x9d in a single quarter, at a time when smartphone sales itself have been either flat or declining. Research firm Counterpoint notes that India accounted for almost 43% of the global feature phone shipments in the quarter on the back of devices by Reliance Industries\xe2\x80\x99 Jio.

The socio-economic situation in India is also driving preference for voice calls. A recent survey by Pew Research Centre found that, despite the push for digital, only 25% of Indian adults use the internet or own a smartphone, compared with 96% in South Korea and 89% in the US.

Jigar Doshi, co-founder, Komparify.com, a comparison platform for mobile plans, says telcos making voice calls free has driven high adoption, making the operators re-look at the plans structure. \xe2\x80\x9cWe have started seeing terms such as \xe2\x80\x98not for commercial usage\xe2\x80\x99 appearing prominently on plan descriptions in recent times,\xe2\x80\x9d Doshi says.

On the flip side, observers believe low prices of voice calls are also contributing to poor quality of call services and the infamous call drops.

Despite increasing adoption, Hemant Joshi, partner for technology, media and telecommunications at Deloitte India, says it is \xe2\x80\x9cvery unlikely that voice services could be monetised by the operators\xe2\x80\x9d, given the fierce competition in the sector. Kawoosa adds revenue from data, too, is still a distant dream for telcos. \xe2\x80\x9cThe data growth is artificial in a way, as it is not priced at what it deserves due to hyper competition.\xe2\x80\x9d


'

b'MUMBAI: Sun Pharma\xe2\x80\x99s lengthy clarification to allay investor fears on January 22, has given rise to more concerns, with shareholder advisory firms seeking an independent enquiry into allegations of "questionable\xe2\x80\x9d corporate governance practices. The company\xe2\x80\x99s board should address the larger issue of corporate governance, and not initiate a knee-jerk reaction for dealing with immediate concerns raised by the whistleblower, they feel. 

The company\xe2\x80\x99s board needs to step in and review veracity of the allegations, and ask for an independent enquiry to get clarity on these issues, the firms\xe2\x80\x99 say. Last week, a second whistleblower complaint was reportedly made to markets regulator Sebi against Sun Pharma about serious corporate governance lapses. In the 172-page letter to Sebi, the whistleblower has detailed how loans were given to various companies related to the promoters. The first whistleblower complaint against Sun Pharma was sent to Sebi in November 2018, which was followed by a note by foreign broking house Macquarie.



