        The complaint forced Sun Pharma to come out with a detailed clarification on January 22, mentioning certain corrective action, while it denied it had advanced any loan to Suraksha Realty, a company owned by Sudhir Valia, the brother-in-law of Dilip Shanghavi, promoter of the drugs major.

The company appears to have addressed the immediate issues that plague investors (through the announcement), but it remains silent on how it proposes to correct some of its corporate governance practices. \xe2\x80\x9cThe environment today is far more focussed on long-term value, and that needs to be the concentration of its management, promoters, and the board. Sun Pharma\xe2\x80\x99s board has failed to control the narrative. It needs to rethink its corporate governance structures\xe2\x80\x9d, says a note by Institutional Investor Advisory Services (IiAS). 

Sun Pharma is today weighed down not by its operations or the fallout of its Ranbaxy merger, but corporate governance issues which are of its own making. We would like to see Sun Pharma's independent directors institute an independent investigation into the whistleblower allegations, according to Hetal Dalal, COO of IiAS.

The company\xe2\x80\x99s clarification has clearly established that a major part of the complaint by whistleblower is true, noted JN Gupta MD, Stakeholders Empowerment Services. \xe2\x80\x9cThe clarification has raised more questions --mainly about lack of disclosure, while a web of related party transactions have been brought to the attention of shareholders. Also, structure of the company being complex further confounds investors. There is a need for investors to probe deeper and ask questions even though things may seem fine (on the surface)\xe2\x80\x9d, he told TOI.

When contacted a Sun Pharma spokesperson, said: We are not aware about any shareholder proxy firms seeking an independent enquiry. In our last investors\xe2\x80\x99 call on Dec 3, 2018, our investors expressed some concerns regarding certain business arrangements and we addressed these concerns via our \xe2\x80\x98Investor Update\xe2\x80\x99 shared with exchanges. If still there are any concerns that our investors or other stakeholders have, we are happy to understand those and are committed to address them in the most transparent manner.\xe2\x80\x9d

The silence of Sun Pharma\xe2\x80\x99s independent directors on the issue has also raised eyebrows. This may perhaps be a function of how the board and board committees are structured -- four employees of Sun Pharma group, two of which are promoters (Shanghvi and Valia), while the chairperson, Israel Makov is not considered independent because his company appears to have business dealings with the company, the IiAS note adds. The independent directors include Vivek Chaand Sehgal, Gautam Doshi and Rekha Sethi.

Though on questions being raised regarding the company\xe2\x80\x99s auditors, Valia & Timbadia, the company had announced that it will induct SRBC, IiAS feels there needs to be oversight across the group given the complexity of the business structure and concerns over quality of audit in subsidiaries.

Regarding the company\xe2\x80\x99s move to transition domestic formulations distribution to one of its wholly-owned subsidiaries, replacing Aditya Medisales, the larger question being asked now is about the rationale for this structure. According to the whistle-blower, Aditya Medisales had the same address as that of Sun Pharma for several years \xe2\x80\x93 and this casts aspersions. As regards the amount of Rs 2,238 crore reported as loans and advances on March 31, 2018, the nature and ownership of Altas Global Trading is unclear, as is its rationale for assuming the liability on behalf of the company, the firm said. 

'

