The quarterfinal played at this venue a few days back was done and dusted within a few hours on day three. Gujarat, chasing 194 runs, were bowled out for 81. The trio of Kerala pacers \xe2\x80\x93 Basil Thampi, Sandeep Warrier and MD Nidheesh \xe2\x80\x93 picked up 19 of the 20 wickets. 

It is being said that the pitch for the semifinal would be less harsh on the batters. Though it might have been music to his ears, Vidarbha skipper Faiz Fazal talked about not being bothered about the pitch. 

\xe2\x80\x9cWhenever we are in the middle, we are very confident about our abilities and that\xe2\x80\x99s what the preparation is all about. We are well prepared and we don\xe2\x80\x99t really care about how the wicket is,\xe2\x80\x9d Fazal said ahead of the semifinal. 

With the pitch having less bite, it\xe2\x80\x99s advantage Vidarbha. The Kerala pace attack has been in menacing form this season. 

\xe2\x80\x9cWe have the best bowling attack in the country. So I think we can counter their batting,\xe2\x80\x9d Kerala skipper Sachin Baby said on the eve of the match. 

With Umesh Yadav and Rajneesh Gurbani back in the Vidarbha fold, the pace attack of the two teams looks at par. It remains to be seen whether coach Chandrakant Pandit goes with Suniket Bingewar as the third seamer. The other fast-bowling options that the coach has at his disposal \xe2\x80\x93 Aditya Thakare and Yash Thakur \xe2\x80\x93 had strenuous nets session on Wednesday. 

When it comes to batting department, Vidarbha look far ahead. With Sanjay Ramaswamy scoring a ton in the last match, the opening combination looks settled. Skipper Fazal, with three centuries under his belt, has been giving Vidarbha solid starts. Ageless Wasim Jaffer has lorded over the middle-order. The veteran has so far amassed 969 runs in nine matches with the help of four hundreds. 

The likes of Ganesh Satish and Mohit Kale have been among the runs. Akshay Wadkar also has been in good form. The wicketkeeper-batsman has so far scored three centuries to pile up 680 runs in the season. Fazal sounded confident about his batters coming good in the semifinal. 

\xe2\x80\x9cIn every game, we think about batting only once. Our aim will be the same again in this game,\xe2\x80\x9d he said.

In contrast, Kerala batting hasn\xe2\x80\x99t fired on all cylinders. Barring skipper Baby and allrounder Jalaj Saxena, others in the Kerala line-up haven\xe2\x80\x99t had a consistent run. The opening combination has also been changed many times in the nine matches. 

The hosts will also have to deal with the sense of occasion. They are in the last-four stage of the Ranji Trophy for the first time. Coach Dav Whatmore has cautioned his players about \xe2\x80\x98avoiding the obvious distractions\xe2\x80\x99. 

'

