In world No. 13 Fognini\xe2\x80\x99s absence, Marco Cecchinato will be spearheading Italy\xe2\x80\x99s challenge. His selection didn\xe2\x80\x99t surprise Bhupathi. "He had his breakthrough year last season after making the French Open semis and is world No. 18 now. So no surprises there,\xe2\x80\x9d the multiple Grand Slam doubles winner remarked. "We know he hits a heavy ball. A couple of our guys have played him\xe2\x80\xa6 I also watched him play a five-setter in the Australian Open versus (Filip) Krajinovic.\xe2\x80\x9d

Despite acknowledging Italy\xe2\x80\x99s superior strength, Bhupathi is excited with the team at his disposal.

"Prajnesh (Gunneswaran) qualified for the Australian Open, his ranking is at an all-time high (No. 109), so is his confidence. He\xe2\x80\x99s a late starter because of injuries, but having broken through last year after starting the season at around 350, he\xe2\x80\x99s brimming with confidence,\xe2\x80\x9d said Bhupathi.

Prajnesh's added advantage is that he is a left-hander. "Having a quality leftie in the team is a definite advantage, I don't think we had one before. Two years ago when we brought him for the Uzbekistan tie, he was ranked 350, but there was a specific reason and it worked for us," Bhupathi recalled.

The captain heaped praise on Ramanathan too. "Prajnesh is our No. 1 player, but having said that, Ram (Ramanathan) on these courts is going to be super dangerous. We all saw what he did in Newport last year\xe2\x80\xa6he should have won the title," said Bhupathi, referring to the 2018 ATP Hall of Fame meet where the Indian lost to American Steve Johnson in three sets in the final.

Bhupathi is also hopeful that rankings won't intimidate his players.

"My boys are experienced enough to know that in Davis Cup, rankings are not always a factor. Both Prajnesh and Ram have played at the highest level, in Grand Slams and Tour events, so they won't be intimidated. These boys actually truly believe they are capable of beating higher-ranked guys if they play well," said Bhupathi, doing his bit to lift the team's spirit.

Tennis pros get to play on grass only in June-July, be it Italians or Indians. "Yes, the boys haven't played on grass for six months, but we feel that's our best chance against them. So we made an effort to come here early and get used to the surface\xe2\x80\xa6 We can't obviously go to South Club now as the courts will be ruined, so we are very thankful to Gymkhana for allowing us to use their grasscourts," said Bhupathi.

All matches in Davis Cup will henceforth be best-of-three under the new format being introduced this year, and Bhupathi felt that's an advantage for the \xe2\x80\x98weaker' team. "The 3-set matches are better for India as it's an easier ask for my boys to win 2 sets rather than 3 to shock a team of Italy's strength," felt the captain.

Having made his debut as a player at the South Club in 1995, Bhupathi is looking forward to be sitting on the captain's chair at the same venue. "Hopefully we will get good support because that is going to make a difference to the matches on Friday and Saturday," Bhupathi signed off.



'

b'WAYANAD: \xe2\x80\x9cWe were richer for the experience,\xe2\x80\x9d says coach Dav Whatmore when reminded about the thrashing that Kerala got from Vidarbha last year. Vidarbha had won the Ranji Trophy quarterfinal that time at Surat by 412 runs, which remains their biggest victory margin, and went on to create history in the final. 


