However, this is only a temporary relief for Sanjita as the sword of suspension is still hanging over her head. A letter by the IWF legal counsel Eva Nyirfa to the Indian Weightlifting Federation (IWLF) stated: \xe2\x80\x9cOn the basis of the information at its disposal, the IWF has decided that the provisional suspension of the athlete (Khumukcham Sanjita) shall be lifted as of 22 January 2019. The IWF hearing panel will render its decision on the athlete\xe2\x80\x99s case in due course.\xe2\x80\x9d

This means that Sanjita could still be held guilty for her doping offence, which, according to the athlete, never occurred. Reacting to the news, National Anti-Doping Agency chief, Navin Agarwal, told TOI: \xe2\x80\x9cThe case was disputed right from the beginning because it had taken several months for the IWF report to come and then again there was a dispute on the number of the sample. Now, the panel requires some more examining to come up with their opinion as to what really is the case.\xe2\x80\x9d

The panel is of the view that it can\xe2\x80\x99t keep Sanjita waiting for eternity while further examining the evidences at its disposal to arrive at a final decision.

According to Nada officials, Sanjita\xe2\x80\x99s temporary exoneration isn\xe2\x80\x99t that simple as it may look since there\xe2\x80\x99s no connection between her failed dope test and the incorrect numbering of her sample. \xe2\x80\x9cLook, the road ahead is very tricky. All three scenarios are possible \xe2\x80\x93 suspension, reprimand or complete exoneration,\xe2\x80\x9d a Nada expert opined.



