        \xe2\x80\x9cAs part of our alliance with Europcar, you can now book a car in any of the 130 countries where Europcar has a network. And if a client is abroad, he can book a car in India. We expect substantial business coming from this route as India\xe2\x80\x99s outbound travel market is booming and the outbound car rental market is expected to fuel some of that business,\xe2\x80\x9d said Rajesh Loomba, MD, Eco Rent A Car. Europcar has similar partnerships in China with IZU, in Japan with Times, in Canada with Discount and in the US with Advantage.Eco Rent A Car is not the only rental company eyeing outbound Indian tourists. Rental MNC Avis is also aggressively targeting this market for growth. \xe2\x80\x9cTravellers are increasingly booking self-drive cars to make their ground travels in foreign lands stress-free and more adventurous. Avis India has witnessed a huge growth in the number of outbound bookings made for self-drive & chauffeur driven cars since 2016. To facilitate these rentals, Avis India offers self-drive cars at 5,500+ locations in 170 countries, with a presence in almost every major airport across the globe and chauffeur driven cars in over 110 countries,\xe2\x80\x9d said Sunil Gupta, MD and CEO, Avis India. Avis, he added, has witnessed high level of demand for cars at this time of the year to countries like the US, Australia, New Zealand and South Africa.The travel trade is already tracking this new trend with interest. Said Debolin Sen, business head, Self Drive 365 powered by Cox & Kings: \xe2\x80\x9cThere has been an 18% rise in the number of enquiries for self-drive tours year-on-year. New Zealand, Ireland, Iceland, South Africa, Spain and Australia are the favourite destinations for Indians for self-drive tours and couples form the topmost segment of travellers interested in self-drive trips.\xe2\x80\x9dThe overseas rental option is an offshoot of the increasing preference for self-driven weekend holidays in India. \xe2\x80\x9cEco Rent A Car will use Europcar brand and systems to grow our self drive business in India by developing a franchise system and we are actively developing it now,\xe2\x80\x9d said Loomba. Although the self drive is currently less than 1% of the rental business, experts expect it to follow China\xe2\x80\x99s trajectory which has shot up to 20% plus in 7-8 years.'

