PNB (International), which has seven branches in the UK, was suing five Indians residing in Chennai, one American in the US and three companies \xe2\x80\x94 Pesco Beam in both the US and India and US-based Trishe Resources \xe2\x80\x94 in London claiming they fraudulently misled the bank to lend them tens of millions of dollars for oil re-refining and wind energy projects in the US and also claiming breach of contract stating they had not repaid the loans. The loans were extended between March 29, 2011 and December 1, 2014.

In his judgment handed down on Thursday, Marsh referred to a \xe2\x80\x9ccatalogue of procedural failings\xe2\x80\x9d by the bank. He blasted the bank for not admitting to the court that it had concurrent proceedings against some of the defendants in South Carolina in the US and in Chennai. \xe2\x80\x9cThe failure to draw to the attention of the court the existence of the foreign claims was a serious breach of the claimant\xe2\x80\x99s duty to the court,\xe2\x80\x9d he said, and \xe2\x80\x9ca material breach of the claimant\xe2\x80\x99s duty of frankness\xe2\x80\x9d. \xe2\x80\x9cThere is no good reason why those defendants should be sued in this jurisdiction as well as in the US or India. All the more so in the light of the weakness of the claim in deceit and for misrepresentation,\xe2\x80\x9d Marsh said.

Speaking after the case, barrister Karishma Vora, who argued successfully against PNB\xe2\x80\x99s senior counsel, representing one of the defendants, Chennai-based Narasimhan Ramkhumar, said: \xe2\x80\x9cThe judgment reveals how differently fraud is pleaded in India and England. England requires particularisation and proof of fraud else the claim will fail at the outset.\xe2\x80\x9d

