                    // code for showing ctn ad
                     $('#fluid-container').remove();
                     return;
                  }	
                  $('.top2brdiv').remove();
                  $('#fluid-container').append(adContainer);
                })()




The massive difference between the prices
The glaring price gap between a fitness band and a smartwatch is very much visible. While you get a 
Xiaomi Mi Band 3 at Rs 2,000, the cheapest \xe2\x80\x98branded\xe2\x80\x99 smartwatch one could find is the Samsung Gear Sport, which is available at around Rs 13,000 via Amazon India. It was until recently when Xiaomi-backed Huami entered India and launched its smartwatch at Rs 5,000 price segment, something that is more accessible and gives the Chinese tech firm a huge boost in the particular market.

IDC analyst Jaipal Singh told Gadgets Now over an email that one of the biggest factors \xe2\x80\x9cis the average selling price of these bands, which is USD 26 so it is not much for many first-time users to start their journey with these bands. Vendors like Xiaomi were able to gain a quick share in this segment based on this strong value proposition.\xe2\x80\x9d Singh added that \xe2\x80\x9cHere smartwatches were not able to replace the attached value with these traditional watches. Most of the smartwatches were centered around the fitness theme which is not able to attract to users who are attached with traditional watches from their childhood. Though few brands did experiment with the hybrid watches, keeping the traditional looks with new smart features but still, the prices of these smartwatches are not in the range of the mass buyers in India.\xe2\x80\x9d

Counterpoint analyst resonates the same idea but says that smartwatch makers may have understood the game and have started lowering the prices of the wearable (take Xiaomi-backed Huami\xe2\x80\x99s case for instance). \xe2\x80\x9cPrice is one of the major factors especially for a market like India, where the majority of buyer\xe2\x80\x99s purchasing decision is based on a pro-con analysis before opting for smartwatch or band. However, we saw a declining trend of prices of the smartwatch in 2018 and we expect it to continue in 2019,\xe2\x80\x9d says Sinha. 





Getting the basic job done
No only the lower prices make first-timers prefer fitness bands over smartwatches, another reason is the value they bring for the price. They deliver nearly all the basic features in addition to fitness-specific functions including tracking steps, calorie count, measuring heart beats, tracking sleep, showing you smartphone notifications and alerting you on incoming calls. So, this leaves smartwatches with limited extra features. These include making calls (LTE supporting watches), sending pre-fed replies to texts and giving you a larger screen to fiddle more easily. But do these justify the price gap?

Sinha says that Indian while users have not yet made themselves comfortable with the idea of a smartwatch, most fitness bands coming with more features are making customers prefer them instead. \xe2\x80\x9cConsumers have still not warmed up to the idea of adding additional devices, that needs to be charged periodically for almost the same features present in advanced smartband which last more than a smartwatch. Mi Band 2 onward series and Honor band series already incorporated heart rate sensors,\xe2\x80\x9d he adds.




Smartwatches are still a \xe2\x80\x98different story\xe2\x80\x99 
Despite having a considerably wide price gap with nearly the same basic features, smartwatches are still a \xe2\x80\x98different story\xe2\x80\x99 when you talk about the audience and the category as a whole. The market for smartwatches right now is still niche and those buying the device are select few who know what they want from a smartphone.

Huawei Business Group\xe2\x80\x99s CMO Suhail Tariq says that both the categories will continue to grow as the demand is based on the usefulness of the product. \xe2\x80\x9cWhen it comes to fitness bands and smartwatches, both categories of wearables have a dedicated demand. The demand is dependent on the purpose of purchase and the usefulness of the device being purchased. The two will continue to command respective market share in the future as they serve very unique purposes.\xe2\x80\x9d 

\xe2\x80\x9cWatches have a different story altogether. Its still a vanity product and it has much sentimental value than any other device that Indian users wear in their life. More than anything else, it holds a different meaning as per of the occasions, attires, and age of the users. Here smartwatches were not able to replace the attached value with these traditional watches. Most of the smartwatches were centered around the fitness theme which is not able to attract to users who are attached with traditional watches from their childhood,\xe2\x80\x9d adds IDC\xe2\x80\x99s Singh.

We have seen some brands in the past experimenting with smart bands that were supposed to bridge the gap between a smartwatch and a fitness band. But those didn\xe2\x80\x99t seem to find any takers though.





So, what will boost the smartwatch market in 2019? 
There have been several indications given by Google already that 2019 could possibly be the year when wearables rise again. The arrival of Wear OS updates and acquiring a part of Fossil group that is working on a \xe2\x80\x98one of a kind\xe2\x80\x99 smartwatch technology, all these indicate that Goole could finally launch the much-rumoured \xe2\x80\x98Pixel Watch\xe2\x80\x99. Apple, on the other hand, is also rumoured to update its Watch Series line up. And with these two hitting the market, OEMs too will leave no stone unturned in marking their presence in the market ass well.

As Huawei\xe2\x80\x99s Tariq says, the price is the main factor that is carving a path for the particular device in the market. \xe2\x80\x9cGiven the increased awareness levels amongst the target audience adoption of smart wearables are driven primarily by factors such as design, built quality and purpose fulness. The market is extremely competitive and hence, launching these wearables at relevant price points becomes equally important.\xe2\x80\x9d


Building on the idea, IDC\xe2\x80\x99s Singh adds that vendors have realised what has been wrong with the pricing and may come up with a solution in 2019. \xe2\x80\x9cVendors have realized that the pricing gap is big in India. A few vendors have already started launching their devices in this segment. We are expecting more vendors to center at this pace, which will drive the growth in 2019. Also, the continued innovation in the preventive healthcare side making smartwatches more useful for people and this will be another factor to drive growth in 2019.\xe2\x80\x9d

We have already started seeing this phenomenon as Xiaomi\xe2\x80\x99s Huami brand just launched a budget smartwatch recently in India.

Counterpoint\xe2\x80\x99s Sinha says that the rise of sub-Rs 10,000 smartwatches may be the real reason for the market\xe2\x80\x99s growth in India in 2019. \xe2\x80\x9cWe believe 2020 would be an infection point for mainstream smartwatches whereas 2019 we would see it grow by 80% level but mostly driven by cheaper smartwatches sub-IN10K and new brands entering with targeted sub-segments.\xe2\x80\x9d 

So, while last year we did witness some action in the wearable market around the globe, this year could be the year when smartwatches finally become more affordable, accessible and feature-rich as well. Who knows, the launch of cheaper watches may push companies like Samsung, Fitbit and others to lower the prices of their offerings too. We will let time be the judge.


