Earlier, the Heat Stress Scale, which had inched to a reading of 5 by the afternoon, forced organizers to suspend play at 4-all in the first set of the Kvitova-Collins clash. The roof was rolled over the Rod Laver Arena much to the delight of the fans and the 28-year-old, whose play thrives indoors, in controlled conditions.



Collins, the 25-year-old American, the Cinderella story of the championships who matched Kvitova shot for shot early in the match, softened under the roof. Kvitova dominated the tie-break before bagelling her opponent in the second set to make her first major final in five years, since the summer of 2014.

"It just tastes great," she said of her win. "I'm really happy to be back in the final again."

If Collins' was the fairytale, Kvitova's is the guts and glory journey. She was a victim of a knife attack two years ago (December, 2016), the burglar ripping her hand and all but ruining her career.

"I'm still don't believe that I'm in the final. It's kind of weird," Kvitova said. "I didn't know if I was gonna play tennis again. Not just physically, but mentally too it was very tough. It took me a while to believe, the people around me again and especially the men. I wasn't confident to be alone. I remember the first time I was alone in the locker room in Prague in the club\xe2\x80\xa6"


NAOMI SPARKLES

The Japanese pro's progress through the draw here has lent an element of stability to the women's Tour which has had eight different major winners in the last two years. After failing to go past the fourth round in Slams in her first 10 outings, the amiable Japanese won the title in New York. She's also the first since American Jennifer Capriati in 2001 to make successive major finals after winning her maiden title.



Osaka ran away with the first set in 32 minutes, but Pliskova, not the fastest of starters, weighed in to push the match into the decider. The 21-year-old then did well to hang on to her first service game of the set, seeing off three break point opportunities. She broke the Czech, widely regarded as the best player not to win a Slam in the women's draw, in the very next game. Osaka then pulled off another tough hold in the eighth game, before she closed out with an ace in the tenth.

The end wasn't without drama, however. Pliskova challenged the call on the serve that whistled down the centre and Osaka had to pause her celebration and resort to prayer, hands folded. But not for long as the ball had caught the line.

