Why specifically in the US?
There\xe2\x80\x99s a lot of rampant denialism of science and scientific beliefs. Like vaccinations are no good, not believing the data on climate change. We live in a world shaped by science and technology, and this paradox is just too crazy. 


Scientists will always cite facts and logic but what about the role of imagination?
This idea that creativity is not part of science is quite silly. Science is pretty much a very creative endeavour fuelled by imagination. There\xe2\x80\x99s this misinformation that scientists are not passionate, that we are these people in lab coats with a sanitised, objective view.


So, do you spend all day in a lab, looking through telescopes?
No no, I don\xe2\x80\x99t actually look through a telescope at all because a lot of the data I want from space telescopes comes straight to my computer. I have a lot of other interests. I read like crazy. I collect maps and I\xe2\x80\x99m crazy about modern art. Right now I\xe2\x80\x99m doing an AR-VR project on what it feels like to be in space, with eminent sculptor Sir Antony Gormley in London. I also love to cook. But if you tell me, three ounces of something, I don\xe2\x80\x99t have a great sense for that. Whereas if you tell me something is a billion times the mass of the sun, I\xe2\x80\x99ll be like \xe2\x80\x98oh yeah!\xe2\x80\x99


Do you feel science and math should be an integral part of our culture and society?
Scientific and numerical literacy are really important. Often when girls find maths slightly challenging, a teacher will say, maybe that\xe2\x80\x99s not for you. That completely puts off a whole set of people from sciences and mathematics. That\xe2\x80\x99s where we really have to revamp our education system. Teachers have to understand that children are born scientists, all curious. That level of curiosity has to be retained.


What fired your curiosity about the cosmos?
By nature, I was very curious and I won the birth lottery. I was born into a family of academics who let me figure out whatever I wanted to do. I looked through a microscope, a telescope and fell in love with the cosmos. I was also obsessed with maps and thanks to my dad\xe2\x80\x99s conferences in the US, I got a Commodore 64 before anybody had computers in the 1980s. I learned to program and made a star map for Delhi. Things just took off.


You\xe2\x80\x99ve been actively pushing for gender equality in academia in your past role as chair of the Women\xe2\x80\x99s Faculty Forum at Yale.
My field is very male dominated and the demographics really need to shift to shift the culture. The culture of science could do with improvement, and we would have that if it was open to all kinds of people including LGBT persons. My students tell me that me standing there at Yale and lecturing, makes a difference to them. For the first time, a girl was inducted into Yale\xe2\x80\x99s legendary all-male a capella choir last month. That is amazing. I think barriers are breaking down but the pace of change is slow.


Any attempt to level the playing field?
I recently did something interesting. Astronomy has grants and resources that are doled out competitively. One of them is time on the Hubble Space Telescope. I chaired the first double blind review ever in the physical sciences where you don\xe2\x80\x99t know who the proposer is because all kinds of biases can creep in. For the first time in 25 years, the proportion of women getting time was commensurate with the applicant pool percentage. That\xe2\x80\x99s never happened before. I think this success is going to force big granting agencies like Nasa and NSF (National Science Foundation) to go double blind in a year or two. Everyone was waiting for somebody to do it, and see if there was an effect but I was surprised at how dramatic it was.


Finally, a question that haunts us all. Any chance of earthlings getting gobbled up by the black hole?
We don\xe2\x80\x99t have to worry about it at all! (Laughs) The black hole in the centre of our galaxy is way too far away. I think we have more things that we can do to each other to destroy each other than any cosmic object can!

'

b'Andrew Sean Greer was cleaning up after his dogs on the evening the email from the Pulitzer committee came into his inbox. Quite like the protagonist of his book, the ageing writer Arthur Less, he remained unaware of it until a friend alerted him to the fact that his novel \xe2\x80\x98Less\xe2\x80\x99 had won the 2018 Pulitzer Prize for fiction. \xe2\x80\x9cI was most surprised and then I got very drunk,\xe2\x80\x9d Greer tells Pradhuman Sodha at the Jaipur Literature Festival.





