The book has a character who wins the Pulitzer and then you won it in real life. Did you ever think you\xe2\x80\x99d win the award, and is there anything you\xe2\x80\x99d change about that scene in the book?

The Pulitzer committee does not call you. I got that wrong in the scene. They email you to inform you that you have won. But it felt comical to me, like a scene in a play where someone wins a lottery and their life is transformed. Also, I was happy about my book; wasn\xe2\x80\x99t worried whether people were reading it or not. So the right response for me would be to treat it like a funny twist of faith, a miraculous one. 


The protagonist Arthur Less is a struggling novelist and suffers many humiliations as a writer. Did you go through your share of those?
A lot of writers have told me that even at an august experience like this one (the Jaipur litfest) there are small humiliations for all of us. I think because we are basically sensitive, insecure but also arrogant creatures at the same time. For instance, I could get very used to being the Pulitzer winner and being very grand and then trip over the stairs, which I just did, so these things remind me that I should remain humble. 


You\xe2\x80\x99ve captured the midlife crisis, warts and all, in the novel. Has winning the Pulitzer before reaching 50 helped you conquer the fear of getting there?

You have understood exactly! I have no problem with fifty, I am fine. Bring it on. (Laughs a little.) Now I am asking the universe to strike me down, I must tread carefully! 


Was the name of the book and the protagonist originally Less and are you tired of the less is more pun in media reports? Tell us a little about how came up with the last line. 

My publisher wanted to change the title, she was afraid the reviewers would write \xe2\x80\x9cLess is less\xe2\x80\x9d. I said that is not something that I can control. If they hate the book, they would find a way to make a pun anyway. We are keeping the title. So now when I see \xe2\x80\x98Less is more\xe2\x80\x99 I think I kept the right title, it makes me happy.
The last line of the book is an imitation of the last line of Ulysses where Molly Bloom says, \xe2\x80\x9cYes, I said yes.\xe2\x80\x9d Because Less is taking a Ulysses-like journey, I wanted to play with that. I did work on the last line a lot. 


Most Pulitzers have gone to tragic and heavy fiction while yours is a joyful empathetic comedy. How challenging was it to keep the tone upbeat and the ending happy?
In the start I had written a very different novel with a different setting. Part of what I wanted to do was write a book about LGBTQ people that had joy in it. I spent so much time throwing away versions of this book, moving things around and changing words and spent a lot of time on the language of it to make it flow and be musical. When I finished it, I told friends that this is the closest that I have gotten to writing the book that was in my head.


Your Pulitzer has been hailed as a big win for queer literature. Do you feel this will encourage others to write stories around gay characters?
I hope so\xe2\x80\xa6.


Have you read any Indian novelists and do you have any favourites?

I am going to say Salman Rushdie, but it\xe2\x80\x99s not a good answer (laughs). 


Now that Section 377 has been struck down what would your advice be to young LGBTQ people who are trying to come out?

India should expect what is happening in the US, which is a backlash, a step backwards, because a change in law doesn\xe2\x80\x99t really mean a change in the daily life of people, which is (about) shame within families and daily reminders of shame. That\xe2\x80\x99s the thing to keep away. We have to remind each other about supporting each other, because that\xe2\x80\x99s the worst feeling \xe2\x80\x94 of shame when you did nothing wrong. 

'

b'A year ago, there were only 18 Chinese mobile apps among the top 100 apps on Google Playstore in India, and only two of them among the top ten. The number, as my FactorDaily colleague Shadma Shaikh wrote earlier this month, has now doubled. 


TikTok, a short video-hosting social platform, already counts India as its biggest market with 39% of its 500 million global users based here. And it\xe2\x80\x99s not the only app being touted as a potential YouTube killer in India. LIKE and Kwai, apart from other live-streaming apps such as LiveMe and BigoLive, are other contenders for the position of the top video app in India. 


