        Collecting Lego \xe2\x80\x94 yes, the plastic toys made of interlocking bricks that become cars and castles and robots \xe2\x80\x94returned more than large stocks, bonds and gold in the three decades ending in 2015, says a study by Victoria Dobrynskaya, an assistant professor at Russia\xe2\x80\x99s Higher School of Economics.

You might not know this, but older Lego sets are often resold online for many times their original price. In one extreme case, a kit for Star Wars Darth Revan that retailed in 2014 for $3.99 (Rs 280) went for $28.46 (Rs 2000) on eBay a year later \xe2\x80\x94 a 613% premium.

For Dobrynskaya, who wrote the paper with student Julia Kishilova, the inspiration was not about theory.

\xe2\x80\x9cMy son likes playing with Lego and I have a lot of it at home. At one point I thought: maybe I have a ready-made investment portfolio?\xe2\x80\x9d she said.

In a paper titled \xe2\x80\x9cLego \xe2\x80\x94 The Toy of Smart Investors,\xe2\x80\x9d Dobrynskaya analyzed 2,300 sets sold from 1987 to 2015 to measure their pricereturn over time. She found that collections used for Hogwarts Castles and Jedi star fighters beat U.S. large-cap stocks and bonds, yielding 11% a year.

To determine the average yearly Lego return, Dobrynskaya gathered the initial price of 2,000 toys released between 1981 and 2014 and their cost in the secondary market in 2015. The data showed that sets with a relatively few pieces, up to 113, returned 22% per year, almost 16 percentage points more than the group with about 860 bricks in each. The relation wasn\xe2\x80\x99t perfectly linear. Small sets yield the most, but those with 2,000 pieces do better than medium-sized ones.

\xe2\x80\x9cSmaller Lego sets could be more rare than larger sets produced en masse, though it\xe2\x80\x99s hard to know for sure,\xe2\x80\x9d Dobrynskaya said.

Lego sets that focus on Super Heroes, Batman and Indiana Jones are among the ones that do best over time. The Simpsons is the only Lego theme that has lost value, falling by 3.5% on average. Newer sets have higher returns than older ones, though this can be due to a growing popularity of investments in Lego, Dobrynskaya said.

Not everyone is a fan of the theory. \xe2\x80\x9cSomeone is going to find something that is correlated. Purely by randomness that\xe2\x80\x99s going to happen. I\xe2\x80\x99d take it with a grain of salt,\xe2\x80\x9d said Roberto Croce, Managing Director, senior portfolio manager at BNY Mellon. 

'

