        Marriott\xe2\x80\x99s move to buy a slice of Chalet Hotels indicates the Nasdaq-listed, $22-billion chain\xe2\x80\x99s big bet on the Indian market where it operates 100 properties. After the US and China, India is the third country where Marriott has a hospitality portfolio running into three digits. 

Marriott International as well as Raheja\xe2\x80\x99s younger son and Chalet Hotels non-executive director Neel Raheja declined to comment on the report. Chalet Hotels owns five properties in the west and south of India, aggregating to 2,328 rooms, and is in the midst of building three more, which will have 588 keys. 

\xe2\x80\x9cWhile hotel operators tend to move towards asset-light businesses, they invest or provide key money to owners to obtain long-term management contracts. These are done usually in markets that are much sought-after or for prime properties,\xe2\x80\x9d said Sujjain Talwar of Economic Laws Practice. Achin Khanna of Hotelivate, a consulting firm, said, \xe2\x80\x9cGlobal brands usually invest in a project if it has a strategic benefit. It may either allow them access to a key market or enable a strong, long-term relationship with a promoter who they would like to be in business with.\xe2\x80\x9d

Last year, Marriott sold its 33% stake in a full-service hotels joint venture (JV) with Samhi. Though Samhi holds 100% in the erstwhile JV, the outfit owns 14 Marriott properties in India, aggregating to 2,000 rooms.

If the Chalet Hotels stake-buy takes place, it will be Marriott\xe2\x80\x99s only investment in India.

Chalet Hotels will be hitting the primary market this week to raise Rs 1,641 crore through an initial public offering (IPO). This amount will be raised through a blend of fresh equity issuance and sale of shares by promoters. At the upper end of the IPO price of Rs 280 apiece, Chalet Hotels will be worth Rs 5,740 crore, making it the third-most valued hospitality company behind Indian Hotels (which owns the Taj chain) and EIH (operator of Oberoi & Trident) that have a market capitalization of Rs 15,912 crore and Rs 10,311 crore respectively. Lemon Tree, which went public last April, has a market capitalisation of Rs 5,336 crore.

