Mumbai seemed to pick up from where they left before the break. Their 10-day camp in Goa, where the focus was fitness, seemed to have worked wonders for them as they had no trouble in dealing with a barrage of Bengaluru attacks that kept coming their way until the final whistle.

Both teams took some time to settle down, but it was Bengaluru who were beginning to look more comfortable at the start. With Erik Paartalu and Dimas Delgado pulling strings from the midfield, it seemed like Mumbai were going to have to do the bulk of the defending.

Bengaluru\xe2\x80\x99s attacking play almost bore fruit in the 16th minute. Xisco Hernandez failed to connect properly from Rahul Bheke's lofted cross but his shot did hit the ground before heading goal-ward, forcing goalkeeper Amrinder Singh to tip it over the bar.

Machado broke the deadlock in the 29th minute with a move that was superb example of counter-attacking football, something that has become synonymous with the Mumbai team this season.

A Bengaluru corner was headed clear by Arnold Issoko. The ball fell to Rafael Bastos, who took a couple of touches forward before playing a brilliant flat through-ball across the field to Machado, who was making a darting run down the left flank. Machado controlled the ball and took a few touches to find himself inside the box with just goalkeeper Gurpreet Singh Sandhu to beat. His finish was calm and composed, unlike the exuberant celebrations that followed.

After scoring, Mumbai were content playing the ball around and waiting for an opportunity to counter. Bastos orchestrated most Mumbai counters, but the hosts struggled to double their lead. In the second half, Portuguese Modou Sougou, who scored four goals in Mumbai's last game against Kerala Blasters, had at least three clear chances to double the lead for Mumbai but couldn't reproduce his goal-scoring touch.

Bengaluru, backed by their very vocal fans, pushed hard in the final 10 minutes of the match but the Mumbai defence led by Romanian Lucian Goian did well to give them the full three points.

