b'PUNE: The \xe2\x80\x9cpay for what you use\xe2\x80\x9d mandate of the Telecom Regulatory Authority of India (TRAI) kicks in from February 1. However, direct-to-home (DTH) service providers and cable operators are ignoring the details in the new regime, putting at a disadvantage many of their subscribers who have paid for long-duration plans.


The providers are insisting that consumers pick a plan or a bouquet of TV channels despite TRAI clearly stating that DTH companies must honour their long-term commitments. 


