                  $('#fluid-container').append(adContainer);
                })()




As per clause 17 of the Telecommunication (Broadcasting and cable) Services Standards of Quality of Service and Consumer Protection (Addressable Systems) Regulations, 2017, \xe2\x80\x9cIn case, the broadcasting services related to television have been availed by a subscriber with a lock-in period or the charges for subscription of broadcasting services related to television are paid in advance for a specific period by a subscriber in pursuance of any scheme offered by the distributor of television channels, the distributor shall continue to provide such services for such period to the subscriber without any increase in the price of subscription and without altering the other terms of subscription to the disadvantage of the subscriber.\xe2\x80\x9d 

DTH service providers feigned ignorance of the TRAI mandate.

In a statement, TataSky said, \xe2\x80\x9cIrrespective of what packs all our subscribers are on currently, all new selection of channels and packs are mandated to be applicable on a monthly basis as per the new regime. All selection by subscribers will come into effect from February 1, 2019 as per the regulatory order. Further clarity (if any) on any current pack continuation is awaited from the regulators by all DTH and cable operators.\xe2\x80\x9d

Airtel DTH refused comment for the story and Dish TV did not respond to an email. 

However, the customer care executives of these companies, when contacted, said they have no information yet on the long-duration packs and have been told to inform customers to make the switch.

Sunil K Gupta, secretary, TRAI told TOI that there is no question of any new communication when the regulation is \xe2\x80\x9ccrystal clear\xe2\x80\x9d. 

\xe2\x80\x9cNo customer can be disadvantaged by the new regime. If they want to continue with a long-duration pack, they can do so till the pack expires. However, if they find any other pack to be beneficial, they can switch. The choice is in the hands of the customers,\xe2\x80\x9d Gupta said. 

Till recently, DTH service providers marketed and solicited customers to pick their long-duration packs. They also offered up to 28% discount if customers committed to take all their channels on offer for a full year or two. It is estimated that at least 10% of the over 70 million DTH customers signed up for the yearly packs, as high definition channels were offered at a subsidised rate.

Now, the same set of channels are retailing at over twice the price, putting such prepaid customers at a disadvantage, if forced. 

Gupta said TRAI will step in if any of the long-duration services are deactivated by the distributor without consent.


'

