\xe2\x80\x9cNobody will remain hungry and nobody will remain poor as all poor people will be entitled to a guaranteed income. We will do this in Chhattisgarh, Madhya Pradesh, Rajasthan and all other states as well. All you have to do is to give us an opportunity,\xe2\x80\x9d Rahul said. The Congress chief, however, did not disclose details of the scheme.





Rahul\xe2\x80\x99s announcement was followed by former finance minister P Chidambaram\xe2\x80\x99s comment that the party president\xe2\x80\x99s promise was feasible and resources could be found for the plan: something which pointed to consultations on the issue among the Congress brass.

Rahul\xe2\x80\x99s promise comes against the backdrop of speculation that the Narendra Modi government could roll out universal basic income scheme in Friday\xe2\x80\x99s interim budget to calm angry farmers and attract popular support in the lead up to the Lok Sabha elections. The buzz which started after the PM ruled out a countrywide farm loan waiver has gained traction because of the government\xe2\x80\x99s refusal to rule out the possibility of Piyush Goyal restricting himself to an interim budget on Friday: something which led many to think Rahul\xe2\x80\x99s dramatic promise could be a pre-emptive strike mean to steal the government\xe2\x80\x99s \xe2\x80\x9cpopulist thunder\xe2\x80\x9d.

However, the initial response of official sources suggested that the promise of guaranteed minimum income may not be part of the February 1 package. Even as BJP dismissed Rahul\xe2\x80\x99s announcement as a gimmick, official sources said Congress had borrowed the idea from the Economic Survey of 2016-17.

\xe2\x80\x9cIt has since remained on the table because you cannot find resources for a guaranteed income support without doing away with subsidies on food, fertilisers and kerosene. A rollback of government assistance on healthcare, education and employment generation will also become necessary. Else, the fiscal deficit will be in double digits, sparking runaway inflation. Is Congress ready for that?\xe2\x80\x9d asked a senior government functionary, adding that Congress needed to spell out where it was going to find resources from. Economists seemed to share the concern of repercussions for fiscal deficit and the negative spinoffs for inflation.

\xe2\x80\x9cThey have so far followed a rights-based approach. What they are talking now marks a negation of that stance,\xe2\x80\x9d one of them said. Farm distress and anger over jobs are two dominant themes that Rahul has been flagging and the latest statement is aimed at throwing the challenge to Modi.

During its stint, the Congress-led UPA had enacted the employment guarantee law, promising 100 days employment to the poor. \xe2\x80\x9cNow, Congress has decided to implement this (guaranteed minimum income) scheme after its government comes to power at the Centre in the 2019 Lok Sabha elections,\xe2\x80\x9d Rahul said.

Rahul\xe2\x80\x99s comments were immediately lapped up by his party colleagues. \xe2\x80\x9cThe poor of India have the first charge on the resources of the country. Congress will find the resources to implement the promise of Rahul Gandhi,\xe2\x80\x9d Chidambaram tweeted.


The poor of India have the first charge on the resources of the country. Congress will find the resources to implem\xe2\x80\xa6 https://t.co/AGrJsSDFfv\xe2\x80\x94 P. Chidambaram (@PChidambaram_IN) 1548677561000

\xe2\x80\x9cAssuring minimum guaranteed income will result in reducing the growing income disparity. This will put the poor in the country at the centre of socio-economic policies and governance of the country,\xe2\x80\x9d said Bhalchandra Mungekar, economist and member of the Congress manifesto committee. Rahul said Congress walked the talk, pointing out how the Congress government in Chhattisgarh waived short-term farm loans within a day of assuming office when the party had promised to waive agri loans within 10 days of coming to power.

\xe2\x80\x9cWhy didn\xe2\x80\x99t the BJP government waive farm loans? The Modi government said it didn\xe2\x80\x99t have the funds. But BJP and its government have enough money to write off loans of Rs 3.5 lakh crore to 15-odd industrialists and extend benefit of Rs 30,000 crore to Anil Ambani-owned company in Rafale deal. Truth is, there is no dearth of funds but the BJP government\xe2\x80\x99s priority was different,\xe2\x80\x9d Rahul said.

The Congress chief also accused the BJP government of trying to dilute the law for fair compensation to land owners in acquisition cases, and seeking to \xe2\x80\x9cpush these ill-intended amendments through BJP-ruled states\xe2\x80\x9d.


'

