\xe2\x80\x9cEvery day the number of companies and founders one can trust and rely on to build sustainable, profitable institutions is declining. This pool of capital in the fund will act as a catalyst to force businesses to think hard about their choices and offers a viable path to promoters who wish to add value to the communities they work in \xe2\x80\x94 and long-term benefits to their shareholders,\xe2\x80\x9d said Quantum Advisors founder Ajit Dayal.

The proposed public equities fund by former Tata executives comes at a time when global sponsors (called limited partners or LPs in industry parlance) have turned cautious and selective in choosing Indian asset managers. These sponsors \xe2\x80\x94 global pension and sovereign wealth funds, family offices, university endowments \xe2\x80\x94 are consolidating their India exposure behind local fund managers with a proven track record.

Private equity investments in the country topped a record high of $33 billion in 2018, up from $24 billion in the previous year. Global funds have turned bullish as India is beginning to provide large buyout opportunities, or bigger deals. Investors have also harvested exits (denoting profitable share sales) in the past two years.


