                  $('#fluid-container').append(adContainer);
                })()



        The central government has made it very clear that states can decide on a waiver or a write-off provided they have the fiscal space. So far, I have not heard of any state seeking a waiver from the targets laid down in the FRBM Act. I am concerned, although the (loan) figures vary greatly in terms of the overall quantum and the nature of beneficiaries and over what period of time. It should not be that the central government increasingly looks to comply with the requirements of the amended FRBM Act to arrive at an overall debt-GDP ratio of 60% by 2022-23, but the fiscal space so created is occupied by the expansionary policy of some of the states.

In the 16 states that we have visited so far, some have been more or less in conformity with the trajectory of fiscal deficit and in direction of the debt-GDP ratio. Other states have a very sharp deviation. I have to see to the more difficult challenge of how does one prescribe a path of fiscal consolidation, whose direction and pace is acceptable and also practical. For instance, states that are at around 22% debt-GDP ratio are easier to be brought down to 20% in two-three years than states that may be close to 30%. In these cases, the fiscal consolidation will make it completely impractical and during implementation you will need a fiscal surplus for many years.


There have also been schemes such as Rayathu Bandhu and Kalia, which seek to offer support through a different model. Does that pose a challenge to the fiscal situation?

There has been diverse literature on quasi-universal basic income support scheme. All those who have argued in favour have also said that this cannot be in addition to the existing measures and various subsidies. There is a huge difficulty in which ones do you rationalise or phase out and over what period. To do it in addition to current subsidies will impose a huge financial burden that will be difficult for states to absorb or for the Centre to support.


What is the feedback on GST?

Except for a few states, the majority of the states have suggested that GST is still evolving, there have been multiple changes in rates and exemptions and it will take several more quarters for them to come to a robust conclusion on what is the likely behaviour of GST. In many, it is less than what was expected, even in some of the more advanced states.

In some cases, there is the issue of base year, which they believe can give a misleading picture. It is somewhat premature to say what the impact will be in the long run, given that there have been substantial reductions even recently. We would like to watch it for two quarters or so to try and assess the revenue trends. Overall, in the medium term, the revenue implications will be positive, not just in terms of widening of the net but also in terms of an improvement in the quality of compliance, where a lot more needs to be done.


There are areas such as health, education, agriculture, where the Centre is spending a lot of money although these are issues that states are expected to deal with. Does that make a case for changing the devolution formula?

The seventh schedule of the constitution broadly lays down the responsibilities and obligations of the Centre and the states and there has been far greater fungibility in its application. Parliament has been very active. Classically speaking, employment happens in the state and employment guarantee (law) should happen in the states. But MNREGA was enacted by Parliament, food security law is there and then you have the Right to Education. At the same time, it is the fundamental obligation of any government to fully protect India\xe2\x80\x99s sovereignty. Given the geopolitical dynamics and the turbulent and unsettled neighbourhood, it can be nobody\xe2\x80\x99s case that this paramount necessity can even be marginally reduced.


'

