If the grapevine in BJP and Congress circles are to be believed, at least six rebel Congress MLAs, including former minister Ramesh Jarkiholi, will resign ahead of the budget session which is likely to start here on February 8.

The disgruntled MLAs have been sulking after being left out of the cabinet. Four MLAs are said to be in Mumbai, one in Pune and suspended Kampli MLA J N Ganesh reportedly in Goa. \xe2\x80\x9cThe rebel MLAs will come together before the session and tender their resignation,\xe2\x80\x99\xe2\x80\x99 sources said.




'Don't cross lakshman rekha': Congress warns its MLAs after Karnataka CM threatens to quit The Congress on Monday warned its MLAs in Karnataka not to cross the 'lakshman rekha' amid strains in ties with the JD(S). The reaction comes after CM Kumaraswamy threatened to step down following Congress MLA ST Somashekhar's claims that development work had come to a grinding halt in the state and that Siddaramaiah be made the CM again.


The BJP is hoping the resignation of six MLAs will give jolt to the JD(S)-Congress coalition and help it to lure others. It\xe2\x80\x99ll also pave way for them to move a no-trust motion and put the ball in governor Vajubhai Vala\xe2\x80\x99s court.

In the 224-member assembly, Congress has 80 MLAs and JD(S) 37. One BSP MLA and two independents are also part of the coalition but they have withdrawn support to the government. The BJP, on the other hand, has 104 MLAs. To bring the government down, the BJP needs at least 11 MLAs to resign and thereby bring the House strength down and stake a claim.

The state BJP led by BS Yeddyurappa would want his government to be installed at any cost before the 2019 Lok Sabha elections.

However, it appears to be a difficult task. Not only would the BJP need to enforce the resignation of at least 11 more MLAs in order to form the government but the Congress and JD(S) has indicated they are ready with Plan B to poach MLAs from the saffron party.

