b'For a company whose name very few could \xe2\x80\x93 and many still can\xe2\x80\x99t \xe2\x80\x93 pronounce correctly to being the number one smartphone brand in the country, Xiaomi has truly come a long way in India. It\xe2\x80\x99s one brand that gradually understood that at the end of the day in a price-conscious market like India, people are craving for value-for-money offerings . In that sense, Xiaomi gained first-mover advantage over competition like Honor, Asus and more importantly, the brand it dethroned from the top spot: Samsung.

For a while Samsung had the edge \xe2\x80\x93 both in terms of market share and products \xe2\x80\x93 over its rivals till Xiaomi appeared on the horizon. After a few setbacks, Samsung seems to be upping the ante with its latest Galaxy M series of smartphones. Samsung is looking to do to Xiaomi what Xiaomi did to others: offer a value proposition that can\xe2\x80\x99t be ignored in these hyper-competitive price segments.



