PM Modi today will discuss various aspects of examination with students between classes IX to XII and the mandate from the MSCERT is to ensure that every student above class VI is also able to attend it.

Meanwhile, one of the Zilla Parishad teachers from a secondary school in Vijaynagar in Satara said, \xe2\x80\x9cInternet connectivity is weak in our schools and many schools also lack the basic infrastructure. Also, there are schools in the district where electricity is cut off. While the circular mentions that a generator should be arranged in case of electricity problems, it is only a temporary solution. Government should make it a point to provide these basic facilities first. We are also under pressure to send a report within an hour after the programme.\xe2\x80\x9d

Another teacher from Junnar said, \xe2\x80\x9cWhen a mandate comes, we have to follow it as it is. There are many schools in Junnar district where only radio or television screening is possible.\xe2\x80\x9d

The mandate issued by Sunil Magar, director of MSCERT, mentions that each school should screen the two-hour discussion either through internet (YouTube or Facebook links), Doordarshan or radio in case it is a remote village.

It has also been mentioned that areas facing electricity cuts should arrange for a generator. The programme will take place between 11am and 1pm and a report needs to be submitted before 2 pm as a compilation of the same has been requested by the centre by 3 pm.

Along with this, five good quality pictures and a short video should be encapsulated in the report.

The mandate also mentions that in case a school fails to send a report in time, they would be held responsible as the complete data of the district then would be unavailable.

While teachers have pointed out infrastructural problems, educationists believe that compelling students to attend the programme and pressuring the teachers makes the initiative obsolete.

Vasant Kalpande, a Pune-based educationist who has also been an officer in the state education department, said, \xe2\x80\x9cIt is definitely a good initiative that the PM is showing interest in students and their examinations. But when it becomes a compulsion it increases pressure on teachers and students. Every school has a set time-table and infrastructural limitations. It would have been better if there was no mandate and schools were given the liberty to show the discussion at a later point of time. It has now become a mechanical process and will be followed just for the sake of it.\xe2\x80\x9d

Bhausaheb Chaskar, another teacher and a public speaker, said, \xe2\x80\x9cWhen a compulsion is made on teachers and students, the entire process gets a formal outlook. While the discussion is important, it should have been a recommendation instead of a compulsion. There is no doubt that it is a good initiative but the state government has not seen limitations of the mandate. Many schools have electricity and basic infrastructural problems. All these hindrances should have been taken into consideration.\xe2\x80\x9d

'

