Hyderabad, Chennai, Kolkata and Mumbai are other cities clocking highest average speeds. Kanpur, Patna, Lucknow and Jaipur are a few cities clocking average speeds in the lower end. 

\xe2\x80\x9cThe wide range between a city\xe2\x80\x99s fastest hourly speed and the average speed smartphone users experience shows the challenge India\xe2\x80\x99s operators face to support a really big number of smartphone users consuming mobile data services,\xe2\x80\x9d the report said.

Peak hour pressures have historically plagued Indian internet services, and with increasing number of people accessing mobile services and rising average data consumption per user, congestion is only getting more critical. OpenSignal says that in the race to 5G, operators must first ensure \xe2\x80\x9ca more consistent download experience throughout the day across all of India\xe2\x80\x9d by increasing 4G capacity.

Faisal Kawoosa, founder, techARC, believes a robust fibre network systems can help telcos offload part of the data traffic. \xe2\x80\x9cMobiles have hijacked the focus of India\xe2\x80\x99s telecommunication landscape, and focus on fixed line services is needed to tackle data traffic,\xe2\x80\x9d he said.

Rajan S Mathews, DG, COAI, says operators\xe2\x80\x99 efforts to create additional capacity to meet growing demand \xe2\x80\x9chas hit the roadblock\xe2\x80\x9d due to factors like non-availability of sufficient spectrum, shut down of existing sites, and inability to acquire new sites. Despite all this, corrective actions like installation of towers, creation of additional capacity, network optimization etc. are being undertaken, wherever possible, he said.

Kawoosa notes that the \xe2\x80\x9csoftware-defined elements of a 5G network\xe2\x80\x9d will help bring more agility, but till then rush hour pressures is a reality for internet users.


