b'Mumbai: In a bid to attract more investors in real estate investment trusts (REITs) and infrastructure investment trusts (InvITs), markets regulator Sebi has proposed to drastically reduce the lot size for trading these instruments to between Rs 15,000 and Rs 20,000 from Rs 2 lakh for REITs and Rs 10 lakh for InvITs. Sebi\xe2\x80\x99s consultation paper on these instruments, which are popular in developed markets but yet to take off in any meaningful way in India, also proposes to increase leverage limits (capacity to take loan) for InvITs from 49% now to 70%.

REITs and InvITs are tradable instruments that are backed by real estate assets like commercial properties and in infrastructure like roads & power projects, etc. Since regulations were notified in 2014, the maiden REIT issuance is yet to be listed while three InvITs together have raised about Rs 10,000 crore. The latest round of recommendations are aimed at providing more flexibility to these instruments to make them more popular among investors.



