        Samsung followed in the second place with shipment of 70.3 million units. Huawei, Oppo and Xiaomi made up the top five. \xe2\x80\x9cApple was number one in the fourth quarter for the past three years, since 2016. The popularity of the iPhones has helped them with that fete, especially during the launch quarter (Q4),\xe2\x80\x9d Rushabh Doshi, analyst, Canalys Research, told TOI.

Despite iPhone shipments declining by 7% over the same quarter last year, a subdued year for Samsung led helped Apple overtake the Korean major. \xe2\x80\x9cSamsung itself is under attack from Chinese vendors Huawei, Oppo and Vivo, who are launching competitive products at better prices, especially in high growth markets of Asia, Europe and Latin America,\xe2\x80\x9d Doshi said. 

The three new iPhones helped Apple beat other vendors in Q4. iPhone XR at 22 million units was the top shipping iPhone, followed by iPhone XS Max and iPhone XS, at 14 million and 9 million units respectively. Apple\xe2\x80\x99s results for the December quarter announced on Tuesday show revenue from iPhone declined 15% from the prior year, while total revenue from all other products and services grew 19%.

\xe2\x80\x9cWeak macro conditions in some emerging markets was significantly more severe than we originally foresaw, especially in Greater China,\xe2\x80\x9d Apple CEO Tim Cook, said, announcing the results. 

Cook acknowledged that a rising US dollar made Apple products more expensive in many parts of the world, and hinted in the earnings call that a price adjustment in such markets was on the cards.

As for Apple\xe2\x80\x99s continued dominance over Samsung in global shipments, Canalys notes Apple may need to launch a cheaper iPhone or become flexible with channel discounting to safeguard its position. 

