b'MUMBAI: State Bank of India (SBI) exposed text messages sent to millions of its customers by storing the data in an unsecured server. The infirmity in the bank\xe2\x80\x99s systems was exposed by US-based TechCrunch, which publishes technology news.


TechCrunch said the information was available to anyone who knew where to look. The server held two months of data from SBI Quick \xe2\x80\x94 a service that allows customers to pull basic account information by sending an SMS or through a missed call. The text messages sent by the bank typically contain the account balance or the last five transactions. 


