The third lender OBC is comfortable in terms of capital, following a fresh infusion by the government, and has seen its NPA come down below 6%. \xe2\x80\x9cHence, it is decided to remove the restrictions placed on OBC under the PCA framework, subject to certain conditions and close monitoring,\xe2\x80\x9d the RBI said.According to Gupta, although the three banks have exited PCA, the question remains whether negative return on asset continues to remain a PCA criteria? Following the RBI announcement, finance secretary Rajeev Kumar said in a tweet that this was an outcome of the government\xe2\x80\x99s \xe2\x80\x984R\xe2\x80\x99 strategy \xe2\x80\x94 recognition of stressed loans as default, recapitalisation of banks, resolution of bad loans, and reform of public sector. \xe2\x80\x9cBanks need to be more responsible, adopt high underwriting and risk management standards to avoid recurrence,\xe2\x80\x9d Kumar added.

The RBI\xe2\x80\x99s move comes two days after a review meeting of banks with interim finance minister Piyush Goyal. In the meeting, Goyal informed banks that if they maintained the trend of improvement in performance, they would exit the PCA soon.


'

b'Coimbatore: Even as there is growing nervousness among investors about the upcoming elections and resultant volatility in equities, data shows that stock markets have delivered positive returns in the months that followed the polls in each of the last five general elections. The stock markets have delivered returns of more than 15% in the post-election phase regardless of the party that won the majority or the quantum of the mandate it got. The markets had declined about 15% between October 1997 and January 1998 just ahead of the general elections.

But equities recovered after the first NDA (National Democratic Alliance) government headed by Atal Bihari Vajpayee was formed. The stock markets registered a nearly 10.6% increase between February 1998 and April 1998. Similarly, the markets, which had remained flat in the run up to the Lok Sabha elections in 1999, gave 17.1% returns between October 1999 and January 2000 after Vajpayee won a strong mandate for his coalition.



