        The script changed in the next general election in 2004 when the Congress-led UPA (United Progressive Alliance) rode to power. Though the markets had fallen 16% between February and May 2004 and had tanked after the election results were announced on concerns that a UPA government supported by the Left parties would hamper economic reforms and stop sale of government-owned companies, it recovered to post 41% gains in the next one year. The markets had crashed following the global meltdown in equities after the global financial crisis ahead of general elections in 2009. The benchmark Sensex plunged from a high of about 21,200 points in January 2008 to around 7,700 points in October that year. The Sensex slumped 27% in September 2008, the maximum during a single month till date, but recovered most of the losses after the election results were announced and scaled back to its previous peak of 21,000 points in November 2010.

After remaining flat between October 2013 and January 2014, the markets rallied about 15% between February and May 2014 and continued the momentum run after that. The benchmark Sensex crossed the milestone of 30,000 points for the first time in March 2015.

\xe2\x80\x9cThe election year might be a volatile and uncertain period, casting a momentary shadow over Indian Inc\xe2\x80\x99s growth engine. But irrespective of the election outcome, we can expect an uptrend in the markets post elections,\xe2\x80\x9d said Deven R Choksey, managing director , KR Choksey Shares and Securities.

\xe2\x80\x9cThe overhang would go away once the elections are over. The markets would then start following the fundamentals,\xe2\x80\x9d he said. \xe2\x80\x9cSince there is an element of fear (among investors) before elections, the markets are either stagnant or they see a decline,\xe2\x80\x9d said G Chokkalingam, MD, Equinomics Research and Advisory.

\xe2\x80\x9cBut markets typically recover once the new government comes to power as fear gets subdued,\xe2\x80\x9d he said. \xe2\x80\x9cThe fundamentals of the economy remain strong. We would not see any major change unless the coalition is a very bad one,\xe2\x80\x9d Choksey stated.


'

