        It was at a trade fair in 1967 in New Delhi when the \xe2\x80\x98Iron Lady of India\xe2\x80\x99 spent more than half-an-hour watching him work and asking questions like an apprentice while beaming smiles in between. \xe2\x80\x9cShe was an inquisitive person,\xe2\x80\x9d recounts Siddiqui, who was 25 then.


It was a defining moment for Khaleq \xe2\x80\x93 Indira Gandhi at a touching distance and intrigued by Bidri work. Vintage images encased in photo frames inside \xe2\x80\x98Bidri Crafts\xe2\x80\x99 testify to this. In a rare photograph, Sonia Maino, probably her early 20s, can be seen observing the goings-on from afar with her mother-in-law encircled by craftsmen.

For the Siddiquis, it was not the first tryst with the Nehru-Gandhi family. Khaleq\xe2\x80\x99s father Ghulam Gaffar Siddiqui, a master Bidri craftsman of his time, met India\xe2\x80\x99s first PM Jawaharlal Nehru sometime in the mid-50s at an industrial exhibition at Public Gardens, Nampally, where the senior Siddiqui had showcased his collection. Nehru, Khaleq recalls, was impressed.

At 77, Khaleq\xe2\x80\x99s hands continue to craft Bidri items decades after his first lessons at his father\xe2\x80\x99s workshop \xe2\x80\x98Taj Deccan Bidri Works\xe2\x80\x99 in Bidar as a 15-year-old. He speaks enthusiastically about how the 600-year-old metal handicraft flourished in Bidar and was brought to Hyderabad by the Nizams. 

\xe2\x80\x9cThe original Bidri craftsmen use special soil from Bidar, which is rich in \xe2\x80\x98saltpetre\xe2\x80\x99, that darkens the base metal while retaining the radiance of silver filigree work. The alloy has to be 94% zinc and 6% copper, which is first cast and shaped into stunning forms. Intricate designs are then chiselled on the art ware,\xe2\x80\x9c explains Khaleq.

His father shifted to Hyderabad after Bidar was merged with Karnataka during states reorganisation in 1956 and set up \xe2\x80\x98Mumtaz Bidri Works\xe2\x80\x99 at Darulshifa. Years later, Khaleq renamed it \xe2\x80\x98Bidri Crafts\xe2\x80\x99, which is housed in Gunfoundry.

The Shah Alams, Muzzafar Jung and the Babu Khan family were regular patrons of \xe2\x80\x98Bidri Crafts\xe2\x80\x99. In the era gone by, his father made gold buttons for Salarjung III and supplied \xe2\x80\x98Bidri\xe2\x80\x99 artefacts to the Nizams.

After Khaleq\xe2\x80\x99s son Aslam Siddiqui joined the business, a steady flow of orders came from institutions like RBI, ISB and even corporates. The family also designed special \xe2\x80\x98Bidri\xe2\x80\x99 themed restaurants, from coffee tables to door handles and mirrors, for the Marriott and Golconda hotels. For the Ram Charan Tej-Upasana Kamineni engagement in 2011, they made customised table trays to be given as gifts.

US and European countries are major export markets for \xe2\x80\x98Bidri Crafts\xe2\x80\x99 though demand has declined due to high silver prices that hover around Rs 41,000 per kg, says Aslam. 

The management graduate also unlocked the potential of e-commerce by offering the products online and to further tap global markets is planning to set up kiosks or retail stores in Canada and New York where his relatives live.


