One crucial concession in the Budget was for second homebuyers who were forced to pay tax even if they had not rented out their second homes. The notional rent charged on the second home will now be exempted.

 Buyers were earlier reluctant to invest in a second home because even if it was self-occupied, they would still have to pay tax on notional rent. Property experts said the exemption would incentivise the second-home market. 

To encourage affordable housing, benefits under Section 80-IBA of the Income Tax Act will be extended for one more year for housing projects approved till March 31, 2020. Under this section, builders do not pay I-T for affordable housing projects approved on or after June 1, 2016.

 The TDS threshold for deduction of tax on rent has been proposed to be increased from Rs 1,80,000 to Rs 2,40,000 a year. Period of exemption of tax on unsold flats has been increased from one year to two years, from the end of the year in which the project is completed. This will bring relief to beleaguered builders, especially in metros. \xe2\x80\x9cThis will allow developers to concentrate on sales strategies,\xe2\x80\x99\xe2\x80\x99 said Knight Frank India chairman and MD Shishir Baijal.

 Another big benefit is the saving on capital gains tax if property is sold and the funds quickly reinvested in another. The new proposal extends it by allowing the homeowner to invest in two properties. \xe2\x80\x9cThe benefit of rollover of capital gains under Sec 54 of the Income Tax Act will be increased from investment in one residential house to two residential houses for a taxpayer having capital gains of up to Rs 2 crore. This benefit can be availed of once in a lifetime,\xe2\x80\x99\xe2\x80\x99 the FM said. 

'

