                     $('#fluid-container').remove();
                     return;
                  }	
                  $('.top2brdiv').remove();
                  $('#fluid-container').append(adContainer);
                })()



        The target is to achieve 18% return on equity (ROE) for 2022. ROE beyond a certain number does not make sense as it will be sacrificing the future to some extent. The NPA (non-performing asset) cycle has not reached bottom as new names (of defaulters) keep coming up. The majority of the NPAs has come from the 2010-12 period. What gives us confidence is that losses are much lower on subsequent loans. My view is that NPAs are trending toward normalised levels. But that alone will not get up to 18% ROE. We need to make portfolio choices, reposition businesses and get a larger share of flows, and probably make some cost-reductions. But unless you aspire to be best in class, how will you get there?

When do you see the bank\xe2\x80\x99s corporate book getting back in shape?

During (previous CEO) Shikha Sharma\xe2\x80\x99s time itself, there was a clear pivoting of the corporate loan book. We said we will do more working capital loans than term loans and 85% of lending was to A-rated companies. A lot of work has been done over the last three years on risk management and early warning systems. We have a Rs 30,000-crore stock of NPAs and we have provided Rs 17,000 crore towards them. Our provision coverage has gone up to 75%. We do not plan to sell NPAs as we do not see it necessary to sell at a discount merely to get rid of them. Any decision to sell will be transactional rather than strategic.

What steps are you taking to tackle operational risks?

We have had more than a fair share of operational issues. We have hard-wired a lot of controls in the system. Second, over the last couple of years, the tone from the top has changed \xe2\x80\x94 that we cannot afford any operational risk issues. The idea is to look behind all our processes and see how we build control reports to ensure that we are monitoring data on a regular basis. Third is to say no to certain kinds of business. Fourth is we are beefing up our teams on the process excellence side. We will continue to use technology to automate customer journey rather than just hard-wire the controls. We still need to close some areas from an audit and control perspective.

There is a feeling that an outside CEO brings in a culture of his previous organisation\xe2\x80\xa6

I have worked in Infosys and HDFC in the past and both the groups are very clear on a couple of things. One is that growth cannot come at the cost of profitability, and the brand is very important. Infosys used to believe in under-promise and over-deliver. HDFC is seen as conservative, trustworthy. I have learnt and seen these things at both organisations. That is not a culture issue that is a thought process. That is why we have added sustainability to our strategy. We want stakeholders to start believing that there will be consistent performance quarter on quarter because the framework and capability to deliver that has been built.

So you aim to be a boring bank?

People value boring consistency a lot and they are willing to pay a premium for it. They also reward innovation, but that innovation has to support a better but boring, consistent return.

At HDFC Life, you proposed to merge with Max Life. Now with Axis being a distributor for Max Life, would you look at deepening the relationship?

That is a question that Axis Bank has been asking itself for some time. We have very good relations with all our partners. We have a unique opportunity, given our distribution franchise, to get into a long-term relationship with partners in life, general and health insurance. We should in the next six to 12 months conclude on that and move on with life because we are leaving money on the table. There are various alternatives and let us see what comes on the table. If we are getting into a near-permanent, long-term relationship, we will need to be compensated for it.

What are your plans for retail?

We want to continue to grow our branches. We have been adding around 400 branches a year and want to get to 5,000 branches (from nearly 4,000 currently) so that we have a presence across the country where our brand can be seen and felt. We also believe that in comparison to wholesale, retail can grow faster. While the form of the branch can change, it is very important to be physically present from the deposit perspective. There is an agreement in the board on that.


'

b'Mumbai: Rating agency Moody\xe2\x80\x99s has forecast bright prospects for the non-life industry, thanks to liberalisation of reinsurance and the Ayushman Bharat programme adding depth to the insurance market.

