b'With the government announcing a substantially higher gross borrowing figure for fiscal 2020, the bond market felt the jitters with the benchmark yield on 10-year government gilts hardening by 12 basis points (100bps = 1 percentage point) to close at 7.61%. The development may also push the RBI\xe2\x80\x99s monetary policy committee, which is set to meet next week, to move to a neutral stance from its current stance of calibrated tightening.

On Friday, the FM said that next fiscal, the government\xe2\x80\x99s gross borrowing will be Rs 7.1 lakh crore, up from the revised estimate of Rs 5.7 lakh crore for the current fiscal. These could lead to net borrowings of Rs 4.73 lakh crore against Rs 4.23 lakh crore this year. According to a note by Franklin Templeton Mutual Fund, a higher gross borrowing in FY20 negatively surprised the bond markets, as reflected in the 10-year yield moving up by 12 basis points post the announcement. \xe2\x80\x9cDeviation from the fiscal glide path may lead to continued volatility in bond yields,\xe2\x80\x9d the fund house said. \xe2\x80\x9cAdditionally, dovish outlook by US Fed, ECB and BoE may provide some relief to the market,\xe2\x80\x9d it said.Market players also said that rationalisation of stamp duty on all financial instruments is also positive for the whole of financial markets, including the bond market. 



