Goyal said that a lot of things could not be done in the interim Budget but the focus was on addressing the challenges confronting the economy, especially those belonging to the lower strata of the society and the middle class.

\xe2\x80\x9cA lot of people from big families, sitting in air-conditioned rooms don\xe2\x80\x99t understand the problems of someone like a small farmer who has half an acre of land,\xe2\x80\x9d the minister said in what was a reference to the Congress party bosses. He said the income support scheme of Rs 6,000 to farmers was meant to address the problems being faced by the small and marginal farmers.

He also hit out at the Congress for its criticism of the scheme. \xe2\x80\x9cAs this scheme would proceed, it would spend Rs 7.5 lakh crore over the next 10 years on farmers. Compare this with Rs 52,000 crore in 10 years of the UPA,\xe2\x80\x9d he said, adding that UPA\xe2\x80\x99s loan waiver had not reached all the beneficiaries.

Goyal and his team of officers also dismissed suggestions that the government had under-provided for the schemes or subsidies and there would be a deviation from the fiscal deficit target. \xe2\x80\x9cWe have provided for all that is required for oil and fertiliser. There are always some unpaid bills, which will be cleared when they come,\xe2\x80\x9d economic affairs secretary Subhash Garg said. Almost immediately, the minister slammed UPA for leaving behind unpaid bills of Rs 1.6 lakh crore.

