Strangely enough, the Indian team took a detour to Wellington, the venue of the fifth and final One-Day International against New Zealand, from Hamilton, where the fourth ODI was played on Thursday. The otherwise 90-minute journey by a flight took about four hours for the team as they travelled by bus from Hamilton to Auckland and then took a flight to Wellington.


REASONS UNKNOWN

No one in the BCCI or associated with the team was willing to provide a reason for such a roundabout trip but speculation was that some important member of the Indian entourage was not comfortable taking the turbo flight between the two cities. The New Zealand players, with all their spouses, took the flight and covered the distance in 90 minutes.

A team source, on the condition of anonymity, said there was no discomfort in the travel yesterday and he has no personal knowledge if any member of the squad felt the journey was taxing. "The bus journey was one hour, 45 minutes and the flight from Auckland to Wellington was just about an hour and there was a little rest in the airport lounge. The drive from Wellington airport to the hotel is just 10 minutes," the team member said. Reminded about the experience in England, he said New Zealand is different from England.

The logistics are taken care of by the BCCI's operations wing although and the players are consulted before the bookings are done. Virat Kohli is not part of the current tour party as the India skipper has left the team after the third ODI but the bookings, as is the practice, were done much before and it is unlikely he would not be aware of this.

The New Zealand entourage took the turbo flight, which is considered a little slow in the Kiwi land but the Indian team, on the road since November, would still have saved more than two hours on the journey. But more important than the time factor is the question that, was there any particular reason to travel the roundabout way?

The fifth and final ODI will be played at the Westpac Stadium in Wellington on Sunday. India have already have clinched the series by winning the first three ODIs before suffering a rare batting collapse in the fourth ODI at the Seddon Park in Hamilton on Thursday.

