In the afterglow of the Supreme Court\xe2\x80\x99s 377 verdict, this edition of the MGWI contest drew a variety of participants including Varun Shinde, a bearded college principal from Pune who performed in a nine-yard sari; Arjun Das, a fashion designer from Kolkata whose rainbow-coloured drape disappeared into a prism sitting on his shoulder because \xe2\x80\x9cwhen rainbow colours enter a prism, they convert into white light which is the ultimate truth\xe2\x80\x9d, and Santhal dress-clad Prashant Jha, an automobile company employee from Jharkhand, who has faced enough comments on his hometown\xe2\x80\x99s streets to believe: \xe2\x80\x9cCriminals can roam freely in Bihar. We can\xe2\x80\x99t.\xe2\x80\x9d

Unlike mainstream contests, these ramps are unrestrained by barriers of age, height, colour and physicality. \xe2\x80\x9cWe are not going to force you to get an eight-pack,\xe2\x80\x9d says Sushant Divgikar, national producer of MGWI, launched in India in 2014. He even recalls some winners being 5ft-4 inches tall.

The title comes with tangible advantages too. Ever since he became the youngest winner in 2016, 23-year-old Anwesh Sahoo has got work as a designer at Godrej Culture Lab, a Medal of Pride honour, chief guest invites from software companies, four TEDx speeches and even a picture of him winning the sash splashed across the website of the engineering college he had passed out from. All through his days in that college, Sahoo did well in exams to camouflage his sexuality the same way he used GI Joes to mask his love of Barbies growing up in a small Odisha town. \xe2\x80\x9cIt is as difficult and beautiful to be gay in India as it is to be human in India,\xe2\x80\x9d says Sahoo in one of his TEDx speeches on YouTube, under which he sometimes finds derogatory comments that make him realise: \xe2\x80\x9cGay is still a cuss word in India.\xe2\x80\x9d

But there are signs of \xe2\x80\x9cinclusivity\xe2\x80\x9d in the fashion world. \xe2\x80\x9cConversations around size, gender and sex are getting more mainstream,\xe2\x80\x9d says Jaspreet Chandok, vice president and head of fashion at IMG Reliance, which organises Lakme Fashion Week. The LFW has had gender-neutral models walk the ramp in shows such as the Drag Show by Chola and its find, transgender model Anjali Lama, is now a well-known face globally. \xe2\x80\x9cHaving these conversations at LFW doesn\xe2\x80\x99t feel like a risk anymore and we are very mindful that if these conversations are handled with care, then they can lead to a larger societal change,\xe2\x80\x9d adds Chandok. 

No one understands the sheer struggle of organising a trans pageant more than 40-year-old Reena Rai, a Delhi-based activist fondly known as \xe2\x80\x9cmaa\xe2\x80\x9d to many in the transgender community. \xe2\x80\x9cAre you a transgender?\xe2\x80\x9d a local politician had asked Rai when she requested him to fund a pageant for trans women in November 2016. Many have doubted the intentions of this entrepreneur who decided to make \xe2\x80\x9ceradicating transphobia\xe2\x80\x9d through a pageant her mission. So far, she has conducted two Trans Queen pageants in Delhi and Mumbai and is now mulling a Trans King International in Delhi.

Namitha Marimuthu \xe2\x80\x94 whose parents had sent her to a mental asylum in Tamil Nadu as a teen which she escaped by probing a hole through its wall with a nail for months \xe2\x80\x94 dropped over 70kg to become second runner-up at Miss TransQueen India 2018. Not only has she got offers from a TV reality show called \xe2\x80\x98Kings of Dance\xe2\x80\x99, but even landed a leading role as a journalist in an upcoming Tamil film titled Nadodigal 2. Aspiring model Navya Singh\xe2\x80\x99s participation in the trans pageant has resulted in fashion show invites, mobile phone ads and paid performances at five-star kitty group shows.

Yet, the ache for mainstream validation remains. Recently, a TV reality show refused to telecast Singh\xe2\x80\x99s story after shooting it. \xe2\x80\x9cWe are more than our sexual identities,\xe2\x80\x9d says Singh. 

However, instances such as the fact that Miss Universe 2018 had a transgender contestant from Spain, fill both Singh and Marimuthu with hope. Someday, they too would like to stand among women who yearn for world peace and wear the tiara that travels. This cry for equality found voice at the recent Mr Gay India pageant when an invitee came up on stage and said: \xe2\x80\x9cThe other pageant should be called Mr Straight India, no?\xe2\x80\x9d

'

