The Mumbai-based artist has also shared her story as part of the project which invites women to send photos of their bust, along with personal stories of how they feel about it. Women don\xe2\x80\x99t need to send nudes \xe2\x80\x94 though she has received some \xe2\x80\x94 and can dress up your bust any way you want, in a bra, fabrics, flowers or even mehendi. They also have to tell the artist in what setting they want themselves depicted \xe2\x80\x94 in a garden, public space or at a fish market. Indu then digitally sketches it and posts it on Instagram.

And the stories have poured in. From small-breasted women who remember being called Miss Somalia, Kaadi (Marathi for stick), carrom board or Godrej cupboard, to big-breasted women who feel that even as their breasts were a source of sexual compliments, they also caused shame, embarrassment and a sense of feeling ugly. Alongside these stories, accounts of changing sexuality and childhood abuse have also emerged, probably providing a catharsis of sorts to the writer. \xe2\x80\x9cI am privy to deeply personal accounts as women divulge thoughts and feelings they cannot disclose to others,\xe2\x80\x9d says Harikumar, adding that she doesn\xe2\x80\x99t tamper with or edit the stories.

For the artist, who also works as a children\xe2\x80\x99s book author and illustrator, the internet has been a platform to both showcase her work and also draw stories from. In 2017, for instance, her #BodyOfStories had her sourcing stories dealing with issues of body shaming, body positivity, colour and gender bias. Initially, she didn\xe2\x80\x99t think utter strangers would respond to her request, but they did, and in considerable numbers.

#100IndianTinderTales, which curated the experiences of Indians on the dating app in 2016, got her press in India and abroad and was displayed at Kunsthalle Bremen, a museum in Germany. But the artist says she doesn\xe2\x80\x99t aim to attract eyeballs or offer any magical solutions with #Identitty.

\xe2\x80\x9cFor me, it is a social media experiment, a way of dealing with my own self, and the works will now be posted only on my Instagram page,\xe2\x80\x9d she says. \xe2\x80\x9cIt is an open project; what people take from it is what they are ready to take from it.\xe2\x80\x9d

