You spent quite a lot of time in India while writing Life of Pi. An article mentions that it\xe2\x80\x99s in India where you found faith\xe2\x80\xa6

I won\xe2\x80\x99t say I found faith in India but I became more sympathetic to religious thinking. I am not a conventional religious person. My background is completely secular. Art, reading, writing, listening to music and seeing great paintings was the way to explore the human condition while growing up. What I discovered in India was a kind of magical thinking akin to art. Religious texts share many of the techniques of novels such as metaphor, allegory, parable. Because of so many religious manifestations in India, I became more sympathetic than I was before. Before, I just knew enough about religion to hate it: the anti-semitism of Christianity, sexism of Islam, caste system of Hinduism. What I saw in India were more positive aspects of religion.


There\xe2\x80\x99s an eternal debate about whether a movie can be better than the book it is based on. Give us your review of Life of Pi the movie.
I can only think of a few cases of movies being better than the books. One is Fahrenheit 451, based on a book by the same name by Ray Bradbury. Another is Blade Runner, based on Do Androids Dream of Electric Sheep by Philip K Dick. Most times, I think, books are better than movies for the simple fact that a book is longer, and it is more work than watching a movie. When you watch a movie, it\xe2\x80\x99s a pacifying experience. You are given visuals, music to tell you how to feel, so it\xe2\x80\x99s a wonderful kidnapping. But no one likes to be kidnapped, we like our freedom. A book is just these little symbols on the page and you have to construct a story in your mind. Because it involves you more it has a greater impact. We like being asked to help construct a story, we like that participation. I didn\xe2\x80\x99t like Life of Pi (the film) as much as I hoped to but it\xe2\x80\x99s fine with me. I don\xe2\x80\x99t mind artistic risks. The notion of a tiger and a boat with a boy, seemed cinematic but it was not. A story works on tension, a relationship. And with a boy and a tiger there\xe2\x80\x99s tension but there\xe2\x80\x99s no relationship. So, I found the movie visually wonderful but emotionally static


In your new novel, The High Mountains of Portugal, man-animal relationships are central to the story. Beatrice and Virgil and Life of Pi also had animals as characters. What do they symbolise?

I\xe2\x80\x99m not sentimental about animals. I like animals, but use them for storytelling purposes. You tend to be more literal towards a human character but when you see an animal character you see it in symbolic terms. We see hyenas as ferocious. Tigers are fierce and magnificent, primates are maternal, zebras look odd. You project things on to animals. This is useful for a storyteller. In Beatrice and Virgil, a book about the Holocaust, I thought about how could I symbolise Jews positively. Jews traditionally have been very clever. So what animal symbolises cleverness? The monkey. Also, Jews have lasted despite relentless persecution. So what animal is typically stubborn? The donkey. So, I used those two animals to represent two positive strands of Jewish identity.


You live in Canada, one of the few countries which still welcomes immigrants. What are your views on Donald Trump\xe2\x80\x99s curbs on immigrants? 

Trump is a xenophobe and a racist. While you always want to have controlled immigration, you don\xe2\x80\x99t want to make blanket judgements like he does. Calling Mexicans rapists and drug dealers is an insult to the dignity of a lot of human beings who want better lives for themselves. You want a middle ground where people can adapt to the new country they are allowed in, and they enrich the country. Canada is immeasurably a better country thanks to its immigrant population. Being Canadian is a very nebulous term. It\xe2\x80\x99s not based on who you were but who you are. I had a wonderful experience in India, in fact, in south India. I was standing at an STD booth and this couple in front of me \xe2\x80\x94 who couldn\xe2\x80\x99t have been more Indian \xe2\x80\x94 the man rather shyly said, \xe2\x80\x9cExcuse me sir, where you from?\xe2\x80\x9d I said I am from Canada and he said, \xe2\x80\x9cSo are we!\xe2\x80\x9d Suddenly, I was talking to them like a fellow Canadian. So, it\xe2\x80\x99s like an act of faith. Once you get the papers, even if you have an accent, and you dress differently, you are suddenly Canadian. There aren\xe2\x80\x99t many nations like that. You could live your entire life in Japan but you will never be Japanese. I like the state of mind that\xe2\x80\x99s Canada


You famously sent books to former Canadian PM Stephen Harper. What would be your book recommendation for PM Modi? 

I don\xe2\x80\x99t know much about Modi, I don\xe2\x80\x99t know if he is as culturally ignorant as Stephen Harper was. For people who don\xe2\x80\x99t read, the standard book I recommend is Leo Tolstoy\xe2\x80\x99s The Death of Ivan Illyich. Because it\xe2\x80\x99s a book that you cannot read and dislike. It\xe2\x80\x99s about a man who realises he\xe2\x80\x99s about to die and how he feels about it, who helps him, who is indifferent to his suffering. You cannot read that book and not be a little bit wiser.


You recently participated in Sanctum Survivor, a project where you had to live like a homeless person to raise money for HIV patients. What was it like to not have a roof over your head? 

Being an overprivileged, white male I actually enjoyed it. I didn\xe2\x80\x99t have emails to look at, I didn\xe2\x80\x99t have anyone to meet, just be with the weather and people. So I found it quite liberating. But it was also an insight into how horrible it is to be homeless, especially when you have been wrecked by drugs, had an unhappy childhood.


What did you do before turning a writer, and how did Life of Pi change your life?
 did odd jobs. I was a tree planter, security guard, dishwasher. Life of Pi changed my life only on the outside. I became busier, wealthier, \xe2\x80\x9cfamous\xe2\x80\x9d \xe2\x80\xa6 but on the inside, nothing changed. The next book I wrote, Beatrice and Virgil, I closed the door on all the noise. Same thing I am doing now. My next book is set during the Trojan War. Creatively it didn\xe2\x80\x99t change anything. I like all my books. They are like your children and like you love all your children, you love all your books. Each one you wrote for a reason, it was a process and very, very defensible and lovable.

'

