Asked about the plan for the sale of loss-making Air India, which was aborted due to a lack of interest, Chakraborty said: \xe2\x80\x9cEven if I want to do it, I cannot. Earliest it can see the expression of interest is June 1.\xe2\x80\x9d The civil servant added that the fourth quarter accounts of the company have to be finalised before putting it on the block and suggested that the government was open to completely exit the airline. Earlier, the plan was to sell a 74% stake in the cash-guzzling national carrier, which many saw as a problem.

While maintaining that the Rs 80,000 crore disinvestment target for the current fiscal will be met, Chakraborty said that Rs 8,000 crore had already flowed into the government\xe2\x80\x99s coffers taking the tally to Rs 43,000 crore. During the next fiscal, DIPAM is eyeing 10 IPOs of companies such as IRCTC, Rail Vikas Nigam Ltd, TCIL, RailTel and Tehri Hydel while the MSTC Ltd issue may hit the market by March itself. 

The government has been slow on strategic sale but the DIPAM secretary said the long process was being tweaked to shorten the timeliness. \xe2\x80\x9cI will not run away from the fact that we have not run the timelines. The process was long, we are shortening. We look at maximum value,\xe2\x80\x9d Chakraborty said, adding that possibility of post closure investigations also deterred officials from going the full length on strategic disinvestment.

The government had abandoned strategics sales, where management control is given, after a spate of controversies but the Narendra Modi government which came to power in 2014 vowed to take up the challenge to revive such sales. But so far the success has been limited.

