b'CHENNAI: Post-Union budget, the anti-BJP rhetoric in the AIADMK seems to be waning. Even Lok Sabha deputy speaker M Thambi Durai, one of the bitter critics of the BJP government, has made a significant climb down.


Seeking to clarify chief minister Edappadi K Palaniswami\xe2\x80\x99s stand on alliance, \xe2\x80\x93 the AIADMK will align with whichever party that does good for Tamil Nadu \xe2\x80\x93 Thambi Durai, in an apparent reference to the BJP, said, \xe2\x80\x9cIf the party (BJP) does good for the state by reconsidering its earlier stand on issues like fishermen crisis, inter-state water disputes and release of pending dues to the state, we will align with it.\xe2\x80\x9d Thambi Durai has been leading the anti-BJP bandwagon in the ruling camp. Another prominent leader who is vocal about his reservations about the BJP is former minister and party spokesperson, C Ponnaiyan. The two had repeatedly accused the national party of pursuing anti-Tamil policies. They had maintained that the AIADMK would not offer a piggy back ride to the BJP to gain a foothold in the state. In a recent interaction with TOI, Thambi Durai had said the BJP would not win even a single seat in Tamil Nadu.


