The 33-year-old Purav, who had in the past won titles with Divij Sharan and had briefly partnered Leander Paes also, said that Jeevan is the ideal fit for him. Though their initial target is to make the main draw of the French Open doubles, they hope to continue for a while longer.

\xe2\x80\x9cWe have planned to work together for the next 10 weeks or so to try and make the French Open cut. I have played with lefties on the deuce court in the past and Jeevan is one of the fittest guys on tour. He is as good as some of the successful partners I have played with in the past and I think we can form a team that\xe2\x80\x99s just as formidable. So I don\xe2\x80\x99t see why we can\xe2\x80\x99t be a threat on world doubles tour,\xe2\x80\x9d said Purav.

\xe2\x80\x9cOur first target is to gel well as a team and find our strengths and weakness and convert that into positive traits. I do well with guys who can return well and Purav is one of the best around, which complements my movement near the net. More importantly, the fact that we are from the same country and are quite experienced on the doubles tour matters when you are playing a long season,\xe2\x80\x9d Jeevan added.

Incidentally, the duo planned to begin this partnership from the ATP Challenger Chennai Open that starts here on Monday. But as luck would have had it, they got a call on Saturday morning that they have made the cut for an ATP 250 event in Bulgaria next week.

Earlier this week, India's Davis Cup captain Mahesh Bhupathi said that the country needed to focus on winning singles now instead of doubles and the duo looked far from impressed with the statement.

\xe2\x80\x9cWe are lucky to be so successful when it comes to doubles. Even now we have so many in the top 100. I would love to have the same story in singles too but nothing happens overnight. And why stop focusing on doubles? It\xe2\x80\x99s not like we win a doubles title in Grand Slam everyday now. We have not made the doubles finals in six years at a Grand Slam (when Leander Paes won US Open in 2013 with Radek Stepanek). So I think it\xe2\x80\x99s all relative,\xe2\x80\x9d Purav said while keeping an eye on India\xe2\x80\x99s Davis Cup score against Italy. 

\xe2\x80\x9cLook, even today, it was the doubles team of Rohan Bopanna and Divij Sharan that registered the only win over the Italians.\xe2\x80\x9d

Jeevan went a step further and blamed the All India Tennis Association (AITA) for not having a proper structure in place. 

\xe2\x80\x9cIt\xe2\x80\x99s easy to say we need to boost singles but unless you have a structure and an organised system, it is hard to replicate what Japan or China are doing. We have the potential but we need a cohesive system with the government and the federation to take it forward.\xe2\x80\x9d

'

