b'MUMBAI: In an open-plan office, a group of colleagues gather for a discussion in the middle of a busy bay. Others are involved in a phone conversation with prospective clients, pacing up and down during their call. These are common sights one witnesses at the workplace. There may be several positives about an open office set-up \xe2\x80\x94 it enhances team work, builds camaraderie and triggers innovations. But the resultant noise is an unavoidable consequence. 


Research suggests that office spaces are becoming noisier, which could impact work quality, productivity and health of employees. According to a study on the acoustical performance of Indian workplaces by Godrej Interio \xe2\x80\x94 conducted among users, space designers and facility management teams in over 100 organisations across India \xe2\x80\x94 a significant 65% people found it difficult to concentrate on their work because of the noise. 


