When asked about distractions at work, a large majority of the users indicated human conversations and noisy coworkers as the most disruptive sources of distraction. \xe2\x80\x9cIn my last year as MD of a large MNC, I opted to work out of my corner room. Honestly, I struggled with it as open spaces are an invitation for distractions. In the world of WhatsApp notifications and funny ringtones around me, I found it a struggle to concentrate,\xe2\x80\x9d says Kamal Karanth, co-founder of specialist staffing firm Xpheno. 

Every time Karanth had to make an important conversation, he had to move out to a room or a corner. \xe2\x80\x9cJust because I wasn\xe2\x80\x99t in a room where this conversation could have got completed without distraction, my productivity levels came down. I wouldn\xe2\x80\x99t recommend this for senior leaders, though it makes for great optics,\xe2\x80\x9d says Karanth. 

As an entrepreneur today, Karanth has been working out of a co-working space. \xe2\x80\x9cDecibel levels aside, the bigger \xe2\x80\x98noise\xe2\x80\x99 that one has to deal with in a co-working space is that of interruptions \xe2\x80\x94 social and business alike. The open and nonprivate layout of work desks in co-working spaces creates an \xe2\x80\x98available-to-talk\xe2\x80\x99 invitation to colleagues and co-workers alike,\xe2\x80\x9d says Karanth. 

Increased workstation densities and benching arrangements, where more employees are working in closer proximity to each other than ever before, are some of the factors leading to higher noise levels at workplaces. While individuals are increasingly using noise-cancelling headphones to shut out the unwanted sound around them, organisations have begun taking measures to curtail noise levels. 

\xe2\x80\x9cWe have sound-proof rooms for phone calls and confidential talks, break-away spaces \xe2\x80\x94 indoors and open to nature \xe2\x80\x94 for having informal discussions, and there are modern conference rooms designed for effective meetings. This way, we have ensured productivity isn\xe2\x80\x99t hampered. We have noticed that non-official phone calls have reduced as people are now more conscious and are following phone etiquettes, overall making a very conducive work environment,\xe2\x80\x9d says Harsh Goenka, chairman of RPG Enterprises. 

RPG Enterprises\xe2\x80\x99 experience of an open-office culture has been quite positive. \xe2\x80\x9cWhen we began revamping RPG House a few years back, we brought down as many partitions as possible. Open office was a huge shift, considering that we had a \xe2\x80\x98cabin culture\xe2\x80\x99. This change has helped us become more youthful and informal and has removed hierarchical barriers,\xe2\x80\x9d says Goenka. 

A loud environment can have a negative impact on the employee\xe2\x80\x99s ability to retain vital information. Several studies have highlighted the possibility of health issues arising in people who are exposed to high-intensity noise for long periods. This can lead to reduced job satisfaction. 

\xe2\x80\x9cDespite detailed guidelines provided by the National Building Code of India 2016, it is true that noise levels in offices are inadequately monitored. In the absence of actions driven by compliance needs, efforts to control noise are often reactive and piecemeal. Our study highlights acoustic measures should be given prominence while designing office spaces to improve employee productivity and wellbeing,\xe2\x80\x9d says Sameer Joshi, an associate VP at Godrej Interio. 

In industry and roles where there is heavy interaction with the outside world, like customers and partners, an open office can be unproductive. However, in IT firms where people are likely to spend most of their times glued to their computers, open spaces work well. Openness, says Karanth, should stem from the culture and not the infrastructure.

'

b'BENGALURU: Blackstone will make a fresh capital infusion of Rs 800 crore in Aadhar Housing Finance and also buy out the 17% stake held by World Bank arm IFC for about Rs 400-500 crore, according to sources familiar with the matter, as it closes its first major financial services deal in the country. The fresh capital infusion will double the net worth of Aadhar and cut its debt-to-equity ratio by half. 


This would also give Blackstone a 100% stake in the company, in which 80% was owned by troubled NBFC player DHFL and its promoter group Wadhawan Global. The deal has valued the affordable housing-focused Aadhar at around Rs 2,700 crore, which has assets of around Rs 10,000 crore, and a network of 316 branches across 19 states.


