\xe2\x80\x9cThey want to control everything in \xe2\x80\x98Gabbar style\xe2\x80\x99. It is a constitutional breakdown. The Constitution needs to be protected like never (before). The fear of losing the polls is driving them to desperation. They can resort to anything. This needs to be stopped, and hence my satyagraha. Only Bengal isn\xe2\x80\x99t facing this threat; this is being faced everywhere. They are politically vindictive. We have specific inputs that they will wage communal tension in Kolkata.... I would like everyone to raise their voice in solidarity.\xe2\x80\x9d

Confronted by an aggressive BJP national president Amit Shah and Prime Minister Narendra Modi, who, in recent rallies, attempted to corner her on the corruption plank, the CM had been repeatedly lashing out at the misuse of central agencies ahead of the elections.

On Sunday, she said: \xe2\x80\x9cHave you noticed the language the Prime Minister used [in Durgapur]? Is this courtesy? He is a threat to the federal structure of the country.\xe2\x80\x9d \xe2\x80\x9cI have been a parliamentarian for long. And I am not used to the language being used against me. I do not say this as a woman. They are bent on destroying the institutions. I am deeply hurt and will stage a dharna till these stop.\xe2\x80\x9d

In the run-up to the general elections, Sunday\xe2\x80\x99s flashpoint has had a rather quick build-up. Political circles see the sudden quickening of pace in the multi-crore Ponzi cases as an attempt to clip the wings of the ruling party in Bengal. The CBI has followed up all leads on the chief minister\xe2\x80\x99s painting sales; last Thursday, the CBI knocked on the doors of Manik Majumdar, one of Bengal CM Mamata Banerjee\xe2\x80\x99s longest-serving aides. This move came less than 48 hours after the BJP national president, while addressing a rally in Contai, alleged that some of CM Banerjee\xe2\x80\x99s paintings were \xe2\x80\x9cbought by Ponzi firm runners\xe2\x80\x9d. 

The BJP, in the 2018 panchayat polls, had made surprising inroads in the tribal-dominated districts in Bengal and pockets in north Bengal. The CM feels with party deserter Mukul Roy helming the BJP\xe2\x80\x99s election manoeuvring in Bengal, he would target police officers known to be close to the Trinamool regime, say sources.

Sunday evening\xe2\x80\x99s move, Banerjee said, was an attempt to muzzle the opposition unity she forged in her January 19 rally at the Brigade Parade Grounds. \xe2\x80\x9cI have never given in to such pressure and never will,\xe2\x80\x9d she said. Within hours of her announcement, the chief minister received calls from Congress MP Ahmed Patel and Delhi chief minister Arvind Kejriwal. Andhra Pradesh chief minister N Chandrababu Naidu, Samajwadi Party president Mulayam Singh Yadav and RJD leader Tejashwi Yadav and even former finance minister Yaswant Sinha endorsed her stand, immediately pitchforking the state issue into the national politics arena. Her party MPs have been directed to raise up the volume inside and outside Parliament on Monday. \xe2\x80\x9cI am grateful to all political parties who have lent support to this,\xe2\x80\x9d Banerjee said.

She announced that the pre-Budget cabinet meeting would be at a makeshift roadside tent beside her dharna dais. \xe2\x80\x9cI have a scheduled meeting tomorrow but will address them over telephone. I will urge people not to step in any kind of provocation. We will stage a satyagraha till this ends,\xe2\x80\x9d she said. 

\xe2\x80\x9cI have no angst against the CBI officers. They are doing as instructed. And they are being instructed from Delhi. The chit funds have been here since 1980s. It was after 2011 that we came to power, we had cracked them down. We have arrested the culprits and refunded Rs 300 crore to those who lost it. But before every election, they start this again and again. What has the CBI done in the last five years?\xe2\x80\x9d she said. Banerjee was still at the dharna site till reports laste came in.


'

b'KOLKATA: A huge police force virtually lay siege to the CGO complex in Salt Lake and Nizam Palace in central Kolkata \xe2\x80\x94 where Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI) offices are located \xe2\x80\x94 at 7.15pm on Sunday. 


The stand-off between Kolkata Police and CBI for over an hour led to a situation where CBI sought an urgent appointment with Bengal governor Keshari Nath Tripathi. Frantic calls were made to the CBI headquarters in Delhi, following which armed CRPF personnel were deployed at the CBI offices a few minutes after the siege was withdrawn at 8.32pm, as dramatically as they were imposed.


