        Under the pretext of visiting the capital to meet Union ministers for funds for Tamil Nadu, senior ministers and chief minister Edappadi K Palaniswami\xe2\x80\x99s close associates S P Velumani and P Thangamani have been talking to defence minister Nirmala Sitharaman who represented BJP president Amit Shah, said sources. The defence minister, who also makes frequent visits to Tamil Nadu, is the BJP\xe2\x80\x99s key negotiator, as she can converse in Tamil with the two AIADMK leaders, handpicked by EPS to hold alliance talks.

The impression being given is that both the AIADMK and the BJP were reluctant to come together for the Lok Sabha election, given accusations by the opposition that Tamil Nadu\xe2\x80\x99s interests had been compromised and the perceived anti-Narendra Modi mood besides controversies and allegations of corruption against the AIADMK. \xe2\x80\x9cBut, the AIADMK has been as keen as the BJP to come together along with the PMK and the DMDK besides smaller outfits for a mutually beneficial arrangement,\xe2\x80\x9d said a senior BJP leader on condition of anonymity. The BJP hopes to capitalise on the AIADMK\xe2\x80\x99s 25% vote share and the popularity of the two-leaves symbol in the eight seats or so that it hopes to contest, said the leader. As for the AIADMK, it hopes to benefit from the combined strength of the regional parties and eventually win back the NDA government\xe2\x80\x99s indulgence in the event of the BJP-led alliance making a comeback, said a senior AIADMK MP.

The fact that the BJP has sought south Chennai with its concentration of Brahmin votes, north Chennai with its population of natives from northern states besides strongholds Coimbatore, Tirupur and Kanyakumari is indicative that talks are underway. BJP sources confirmed that three leaders are making a bid for south Chennai, including senior leaders L Ganesan, H Raja and BJP state president Tamilisai Sounderarajan. Former BJP state president C P Radhakrishnan and Vanathi Sreenivasan are said to be eying the Coimbatore constituency. While PT\xe2\x80\x99s K Krishnasamy is likely to get Tenkasi with its concentration of Devendra Kula Vellalar (a dalit subsect) votes, the PMK may be given Dharmapuri and Arani besides Arakkonam, all in the vanniyar belt. Vijayakanth\xe2\x80\x99s brother-in-law L K Sudheesh is likely to contest a seat favourable to the DMDK.

While leaders like Lok Sabha deputy speaker M Thambi Durai and AIADMK spokesman C Ponnaiyan sowed the seeds of doubts giving rise to conjectures that it was a larger party strategy to distance the BJP, AIADMK sources say their posturing was their personal agenda. While Thambi Durai fears a backlash from Muslims in Pallipatti panchayat in Aravakurichi segment of the Karur constituency which he hopes to contest, Ponnaiyan, who teamed up with OPS during his rebellion, is merely disgruntled, say AIADMK leaders.

Practical necessities and political compulsions, including the need for a strong national poll partner, was a major consideration. The AIADMK, bereft of a powerful leader like former chief minister J Jayalalithaa, felt compelled to keep the BJP in good humour and pressure the party for a deal. Presenting a reasonably strong political alliance is essential to the party to take on two powerful rivals \xe2\x80\x94 the DMK-led Congress inclusive front and a smaller AMMK-led grouping.

